OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Counterexamples-to-the-Hahn-Wilson-conjecture-at-height-two-September-26-2026", "title": "Counterexamples to the Hahn-Wilson conjecture at height two", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Counterexamples-to-the-Hahn-Wilson-conjecture-at-height-two-September-26-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "319", "family_title": "Counterexamples to finite generation at chromatic height two", "abstract_html": "We disprove the Hahn–Wilson conjecture at height two. For every sufficiently large prime <i>p</i>, we construct a connective <i>p</i>-complete spectrum <i>X</i> of exact fp-type two outside the ordinary thick subcategory generated by the specified standard form of \\(\\mathrm{BP}\\langle2\\rangle_p^\\wedge\\). The same spectrum satisfies both localization comparisons \\(L_2^fX\\simeq L_2X\\) and \\(L_{T(2)}X\\simeq L_{K(2)}X\\).", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every sufficiently large prime \\(p\\), there is a connective \\(p\\)-complete spectrum \\(X\\) of exact fp-type two such that, for a standard form of \\(BP\\langle2\\rangle_p^{\\wedge}\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a prime \\(p\\). Write \\(K(j)\\) for Morava \\(K\\)-theory, with \\(K(0)=H\\mathbb Q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A nonzero finite \\(p\\)-local spectrum has type \\(j\\) if \\(j\\) is the least index for which its \\(K(j)\\)-homology is nonzero.</p><p class=\"li\">A bounded-below \\(p\\)-complete spectrum \\(X\\) is an fp-spectrum if \\(H^*(X;\\mathbb F_p)\\) is finitely presented over the mod-\\(p\\) Steenrod algebra. Equivalently, \\(\\pi_*(V\\wedge X)\\) is finite in total for some nonzero finite \\(p\\)-local spectrum \\(V\\).</p><p class=\"li\">An fp-spectrum has fp-type at most \\(n\\) if this total-finiteness test holds for a finite spectrum of type \\(n+1\\). Exact fp-type two means fp-type at most two but not at most one.</p><p class=\"li\">For a \\(p\\)-complete spectrum \\(B\\), \\(\\operatorname{Thick}(B)\\) denotes its ordinary thick closure in \\(\\mathrm{Sp}^{\\wedge}_p\\), using equivalences, finite sums, all integer shifts, cofiber sequences, and retracts.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(L_2=L_{E(2)}\\), let \\(L_2^f\\) be finite localization away from finite spectra of type greater than two, and let \\(T(2)\\) be a telescope of a periodic self-map of a finite type-two spectrum.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every sufficiently large prime \\(p\\), there is a connective \\(p\\)-complete spectrum \\(X\\) of exact fp-type two such that, for a standard form of \\(BP\\langle2\\rangle_p^{\\wedge}\\),\n\\[\nX\\notin\\operatorname{Thick}(BP\\langle2\\rangle_p^{\\wedge}).\n\\]\nThe same spectrum satisfies\n\\[\nL_2^fX\\simeq L_2X,\n\\qquad\nL_{T(2)}X\\simeq L_{K(2)}X.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For every sufficiently large prime, choose an odd \\(M\\sim p^{2/5}\\). Then\n\\[\nM\\geq81,\\qquad p&gt;16M^2,\\qquad\n2\\lfloor M/8\\rfloor&gt;p^{1/3},\n\\]\nso Proposition 13 constructs the required operation.</p><p>Proposition 16 constructs \\(U\\) with finite sections-homotopy in every degree and\n\\[\n\\widehat U\\in\\operatorname{Thick}(\\widehat T').\n\\]\nProposition 23 gives\n\\[\nU\\notin\\operatorname{Thick}(T').\n\\]\nThus Proposition 32 applies, producing the connective \\(p\\)-complete fp-spectrum \\(X_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\) of fp-type at most two outside \\(\\operatorname{Thick}(b_p^{\\wedge})\\).</p><p>Proposition 26 identifies this generator with the specified standard form of \\(BP\\langle2\\rangle_p^{\\wedge}\\). Proposition 33 gives exact fp-type two, and Proposition 34 gives both localization comparisons. Set \\(X=X_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\).</p><p>The conclusion concerns sufficiently large primes. The telescope comparisons are properties of this example and do not settle the general restricted telescope conjecture for fp-spectra.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Lemma 7.8, the independent transport principle of Section 7.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The full subcategory \\( \\{X\\in\\mathcal C:F(\\Sigma^jX)\\in\\mathcal D_0 \\text{ for every }j\\in\\mathbb Z\\} \\) is thick in \\(\\mathcal C\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F:\\mathcal C\\to\\mathcal D\\) be an equivalence of the underlying categories of triangulated categories; exactness is not assumed.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal D_0\\subseteq\\mathcal D\\) be a thick subcategory.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The full subcategory\n\\[\n\\{X\\in\\mathcal C:F(\\Sigma^jX)\\in\\mathcal D_0\n\\text{ for every }j\\in\\mathbb Z\\}\n\\]\nis thick in \\(\\mathcal C\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>An equivalence preserves zero objects, finite biproducts, and retracts. Shift closure is built into the stated condition.</p><p>For a triangle\n\\[\nA\\to B\\to C\\to\\Sigma A,\n\\]\nfirst suppose \\(F(B),F(\\Sigma A)\\in\\mathcal D_0\\). Complete \\(F(B)\\to F(C)\\) to a triangle\n\\[\nF(B)\\to F(C)\\xrightarrow{s}D_1\\to F(B)[1].\n\\]\nThe map \\(s\\) kills the preceding map. Full faithfulness and essential surjectivity transport exactness of represented sequences in \\(\\mathcal C\\), so\n\\[\ns=hF(C\\to\\Sigma A)\n\\]\nfor \\(h:F(\\Sigma A)\\to D_1\\).</p><p>Form a weak homotopy pullback \\(P\\) of \\(s\\) along \\(h\\). It has a triangle\n\\[\nF(B)\\to P\\to F(\\Sigma A)\\to F(B)[1],\n\\]\nso \\(P\\in\\mathcal D_0\\). The pair consisting of \\(\\mathrm{id}_{F(C)}\\) and \\(F(C\\to\\Sigma A)\\) has equal composites to \\(D_1\\). The exact sequence for the weak pullback gives \\(F(C)\\to P\\) splitting \\(P\\to F(C)\\). Thus \\(F(C)\\) is a retract of \\(P\\).</p><p>Now apply this argument to every shifted triangle when \\(A,B\\) satisfy the theorem’s condition. Its inputs are \\(F(\\Sigma^jB)\\) and \\(F(\\Sigma^{j+1}A)\\), so \\(F(\\Sigma^jC)\\in\\mathcal D_0\\) for every \\(j\\). Rotation gives the other two-out-of-three implications.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Corollary 9.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The spectrum \\( Y'=L_{K(2)}X_{\\mathrm{sp}} \\) is locally fp and belongs to \\( \\operatorname{Thick}(E_2) = \\operatorname{Thick}(L_{K(2)}BP\\langle2\\rangle). \\) Its Morava \\(E\\)-cohomology \\(E_2^*Y'\\) is finitely generated …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\) be the spectrum constructed in the proof of Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(E_2\\) for height-two Morava \\(E\\)-theory, distinct from Johnson–Wilson theory \\(E(2)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A \\(K(2)\\)-local spectrum \\(Y'\\) is locally fp if \\(\\pi_i(F\\wedge Y')\\) is finite for every integer \\(i\\) and every finite type-two spectrum \\(F\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Thick closure and duality in the following statement are taken in the \\(K(2)\\)-local category.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The spectrum\n\\[\nY'=L_{K(2)}X_{\\mathrm{sp}}\n\\]\nis locally fp and belongs to\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Thick}(E_2)\n=\n\\operatorname{Thick}(L_{K(2)}BP\\langle2\\rangle).\n\\]\nIts Morava \\(E\\)-cohomology \\(E_2^*Y'\\) is finitely generated over the ring of operations \\(E_2^*E_2\\), and the natural map from \\(Y'\\) to its double dual, formed using the \\(K(2)\\)-local sphere, is an equivalence.</p>", "proof": "<p>Theorem 1, with Propositions 33 and 34, gives exact fp-type two and\n\\[\nL_{T(2)}X_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\simeq L_{K(2)}X_{\\mathrm{sp}}.\n\\]\nThese are the hypotheses of Lee–Pstrągowski [21, Lemma 4.1], which gives local fp-ness.</p><p>Their equivalent characterizations [21, Theorems 1.7, 1.8, and 4.3] then give\n\\[\nY'\\in\\operatorname{Thick}(E_2)\n=\n\\operatorname{Thick}(L_{K(2)}BP\\langle2\\rangle),\n\\]\nfinite generation of \\(E_2^*Y'\\) over \\(E_2^*E_2\\), and \\(K(2)\\)-local reflexivity.</p><p>These conclusions are local. The local reduction and resolution arguments use suitable generalized Moore exponents, sufficiently small stabilizer subgroups, and Morava operations. They do not supply integral resolutions over global \\(BP\\langle2\\rangle\\)-operations or lifts of maps through the height-open fracture square. Accordingly they are compatible with the global obstruction proved in Proposition 23 and transferred in Proposition 32.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 4", "orig": "Proposition 9.6", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\tau_{\\geq0}E_2\\notin\\operatorname{Thick}(b_p^{\\wedge}). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E_2\\) be height-two Morava \\(E\\)-theory.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(b\\) be the regular \\(BP\\)-module quotient with\n\\[\n\\pi_*b=\\mathbb Z_{(p)}[v_1,v_2]\n\\]\nused in the paper; its completion is the specified standard form of \\(BP\\langle2\\rangle_p^{\\wedge}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Thick closure is ordinary thick closure in \\(p\\)-complete spectra.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\tau_{\\geq0}E_2\\notin\\operatorname{Thick}(b_p^{\\wedge}).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For a spectrum \\(P\\), write\n\\[\nP/p=\\operatorname{cofib}(p:P\\to P).\n\\]\nThe property that every \\(\\pi_i(P/p)\\) is finite is thick: multiplication-by-\\(p\\) cofiber is exact, and degreewise finiteness is preserved by shifts, finite sums, cofiber sequences, and retracts.</p><p>The property holds for \\(b_p^{\\wedge}\\), since\n\\[\n\\pi_*(b_p^{\\wedge}/p)=\\mathbb F_p[v_1,v_2]\n\\]\nhas finite-dimensional homogeneous pieces.</p><p>On the other hand,\n\\[\n\\pi_0E_2=W(\\mathbb F_{p^2})[[u_1]].\n\\]\nSince \\(\\tau_{\\geq0}E_2\\) is connective,\n\\[\n\\pi_0((\\tau_{\\geq0}E_2)/p)\n\\cong\\mathbb F_{p^2}[[u_1]],\n\\]\nwhich is infinite. Thus the connective cover cannot belong to the indicated thick subcategory.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Proposition 2.1, together with the coordinate congruence of original Remark 7.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The map \\(f\\) is flat and affine.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p&gt;5\\),\n\\[\nD=2(p-1),\\qquad m_i=\\frac{p^i-1}{p-1},\\qquad q=m_2=p+1.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use the Brown–Peterson Hopf algebroid\n\\[\nP=\\mathbb Z_{(p)}[v_i:i\\geq1],\n\\qquad\n\\Gamma_P=P[t_i:i\\geq1],\n\\]\nwith all topological degrees divided by \\(D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose Araki coordinates:\n\\[\n[p]_F(T)=\\sum_{i\\geq0}^{F}v_iT^{p^i},\n\\qquad v_0=p.\n\\]\nA strict arrow from \\(F_R\\) to \\(F_L\\) has the form\n\\[\n\\varphi(T)=T+_{F_L}\\sum_{i\\geq1}^{F_L}t_iT^{p^i}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal M\\) be the stack of this groupoid on\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Spec}P\\setminus V(p,v_1,v_2),\n\\]\nalso divided by \\(\\mathbb G_m\\) for the regraded weights.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nB_2=\\mathbb Z_{(p)}[v_1,v_2],\\qquad\nA_2=B_2[v_2^{-1}],\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal X=\n[(\\operatorname{Spec}B_2\\setminus V(p,v_1,v_2))/\\mathbb G_m],\n\\]\nand let \\(f:\\mathcal X\\to\\mathcal M\\) put \\(v_i=0\\) for \\(i&gt;2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n\\[\nA_{\\mathrm{Haz}}\n=\\mathbb Z_{(p)}[v_1^{\\mathrm{Haz}},(v_2^{\\mathrm{Haz}})^{\\pm1}]\n\\]\nwith higher Hazewinkel generators zero.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The map \\(f\\) is flat and affine. Both\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Spec}A_2\\to\\mathcal M,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Spec}A_{\\mathrm{Haz}}\\to\\mathcal M\n\\]\nare flat affine covers. The cooperation arrow ring for the second chart is\n\\[\nA_{\\mathrm{Haz}}\\otimes_{P,\\eta_L}\\Gamma_P\n\\otimes_{P,\\eta_R}A_{\\mathrm{Haz}}.\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n\\eta_Rv_i\\equiv v_i\\pmod{(p,v_1,\\ldots,v_{i-1})}.\n\\]\nThe chart sheaves\n\\[\nT_{\\mathbb Z}=f_*\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal X},\n\\qquad\nT_{\\mathbb Z_p}\n=f_*(\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal X}\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z_{(p)}}\\mathbb Z_p)\n\\]\nare \\(p\\)-monic.</p>", "proof": "<p>Commuting a strict arrow with the \\(p\\)-series gives the displayed right-unit congruence, so \\((p,v_1,v_2)\\) is invariant.</p><p>The Landweber flatness criterion applies to each localization of \\(B_2\\) inverting \\(p\\), \\(v_1\\), or \\(v_2\\), and to \\(A_{\\mathrm{Haz}}\\): the invariant regular sequence stays regular until its ideal becomes the unit ideal. To pass from exactness on graded comodules to chart flatness, apply exact coinduction \\(\\Gamma_P\\otimes_P(-)\\), then chart base change. This tests flatness of \\(A\\otimes_{P,\\eta_L}\\Gamma_P\\) via \\(\\eta_R\\); inversion of arrows interchanges the units. Graded flatness implies ordinary flatness by placing a copy of an ungraded test module in every weight and recovering its tensor product in a fixed weight.</p><p>After pulling back to an affine scheme of laws with trivialized grading frame, the fiber of \\(f\\) is the affine scheme of strict arrows whose other end is a \\(B_2\\)-law. The deleted locus cannot occur at that end by the right-unit congruence. Fiber products of affine law charts retain only strict-arrow coordinates and an invertible grading parameter, hence are affine.</p><p>Coverage is checked on geometric points. In characteristic zero, logarithms give strict isomorphisms. In characteristic \\(p\\), for height \\(h=1\\) or \\(2\\), prescribe right coefficients successively. After the preceding equations, comparison of degree \\(p^{j+h}\\) gives\n\\[\n\\eta_Rv_{j+h}\n=\nv_h t_j^{p^h}-v_h^{p^j}t_j\n+\\text{a polynomial determined by }t_{&lt;j}.\n\\]\nIts leading coefficient is nonzero, so it can be solved at every step over an algebraically closed field. Both charts contain laws of the required height and leading coefficient; for the Hazewinkel chart use Lemma 1.1. The arrow-ring formula follows by pulling back the two ends of the law groupoid.</p><p>Finally multiplication by \\(p\\) is injective on the chart structure sheaves, and affine pushforward preserves this injectivity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Coordinate comparison from original Section 2.1 and Remark 7.5.", "statement": "<p>Coordinate comparison from original Section 2.1 and Remark 7.5.</p><p>In the setting of Proposition 1, the Araki and Hazewinkel generators are related by an integral homogeneous triangular coordinate change with diagonal\n\\[\n1-p^{p^n-1}\\in\\mathbb Z_{(p)}^\\times.\n\\]\nThey satisfy\n\\[\nv_n\\equiv v_n^{\\mathrm{Haz}}\\pmod p\n\\qquad(n\\geq1).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Write\n\\[\n\\log_F(T)=\\sum_{i\\geq0}l_iT^{p^i}.\n\\]\nSuccessively subtract the lower terms of the Araki \\(p\\)-series using formal-group subtraction. The logarithm shows that the next remainder starts at \\(T^{p^n}\\); its coefficient is integral because the preceding subtractions were integral. Comparison with\n\\[\npl_n=v_n^{\\mathrm{Haz}}\n+\\sum_{0&lt;i&lt;n}l_i(v_{n-i}^{\\mathrm{Haz}})^{p^i}\n\\]\ngives the stated triangular change.</p><p>Subtracting the logarithm recursions gives\n\\[\nv_n-v_n^{\\mathrm{Haz}}\n=\n-p^{p^n}l_n+\n\\sum_{0&lt;i&lt;n}l_i\n\\bigl((v_{n-i}^{\\mathrm{Haz}})^{p^i}-v_{n-i}^{p^i}\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe facts that \\(p^il_i\\in P\\) and that raising to \\(p^i\\) improves a congruence modulo \\(p\\) to one modulo \\(p^{i+1}\\) prove the congruence inductively.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Proposition 2.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a flat affine groupoid stack \\(\\mathcal N\\) and a map \\( r:\\mathcal M_k\\to\\mathcal N \\) quotienting the vertical central \\(Z\\)-action on arrows.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain Proposition 1 and put \\(k=\\mathbb F_{p^2}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n\\[\ni:\\mathcal M_0\\to\\mathcal M,\\qquad\ne:\\mathcal M_k\\to\\mathcal M_0\n\\]\nbe characteristic-\\(p\\) reduction and scalar extension.</p><p class=\"li\">Subscripts \\(0,k\\) on \\(f,\\mathcal X\\) denote these base changes.</p><p class=\"li\">The proconstant group\n\\[\nZ=1+p\\mathbb Z_p\\simeq\\mathbb Z_p\n\\]\nacts as central inertia through \\([z]_F\\). Fix a topological generator \\(c\\in Z\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a flat affine groupoid stack \\(\\mathcal N\\) and a map\n\\[\nr:\\mathcal M_k\\to\\mathcal N\n\\]\nquotienting the vertical central \\(Z\\)-action on arrows. Pullback \\(r^*\\) is exact and identifies sheaves on \\(\\mathcal N\\) with sheaves on \\(\\mathcal M_k\\) having trivial vertical \\(Z\\)-action.</p><p>The map\n\\[\nf'=rf_k:\\mathcal X_k\\to\\mathcal N\n\\]\nis flat and affine. The weight line descends to \\(\\mathcal N\\), and \\(t=v_1\\) is an invariant Cartier section of \\(\\mathcal O(1)\\).</p><p>Write\n\\[\nT_0=f_{0*}\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal X_0},\\qquad\nT_k=f_{k*}\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal X_k},\\qquad\nT'=f'_*\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal X_k}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The multiplications \\([z]_F\\) extend continuously to \\(p\\)-adic integers on every jet because the characteristic-\\(p\\) \\(p\\)-series has order at least \\(p\\). They commute with arrows and grading and are faithful on geometric fibers of finite-height laws.</p><p>Take \\(S=\\operatorname{Spec}(A_2\\otimes k)\\). Quotient its affine arrow scheme \\(S\\times_{\\mathcal M_k}S\\) by vertical composition with \\(Z\\). The action is locally finite on arrow coefficients and geometrically free by the leading orders of finite-height \\(p\\)-series. Lemma 2.1 makes its quotient affine and the original arrow scheme an fpqc torsor. Centrality descends composition; identities and inverses descend, and source and target remain flat by faithfully flat descent. Descent through the torsor gives the description and exactness of \\(r^*\\).</p><p>Likewise \\(S\\times_{\\mathcal N}\\mathcal X_k\\) is the invariant affine quotient of \\(S\\times_{\\mathcal M_k}\\mathcal X_k\\). There is no residual automorphism from \\(\\mathcal X_k\\) in the vertical subgroup: such an automorphism is a grading element, whose parameter must be the identity to lie in the strict subgroup. The quotient is flat over \\(S\\) by descent. Thus \\(f'\\) is flat affine.</p><p>The central action is trivial on the weight line. The characteristic-\\(p\\) right-unit congruence makes \\(v_1\\) invariant, and \\(v_1\\) is a non-zero-divisor on law charts.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(A\\) be a \\(k\\)-algebra with a locally finite continuous \\(Z\\)-action.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(A\\) be a \\(k\\)-algebra with a locally finite continuous \\(Z\\)-action. Let \\(Z_m\\subset Z\\) have index \\(p^m\\), and suppose\n\\[\nA=\\bigcup_{m\\geq0}A^{Z_m}.\n\\]\nIf the action on \\(\\operatorname{Spec}A\\) is geometrically free, then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Spec}A\\to\\operatorname{Spec}(A^Z)\n\\]\nis an fpqc \\(Z\\)-torsor.</p>", "proof": "<p>Set \\(A_m=A^{Z_m}\\). Every element has finite orbit, so \\(A\\) is integral over each \\(A_m\\). Two geometric points with equal restriction to \\(A_m\\) differ by \\(Z_m\\): on an algebra generated by finitely many finite orbits, distinct finite-group orbits are separated by invariant functions, using the Chinese remainder theorem and an invariant product. Invariants commute with field extension. Compactness of \\(Z_m\\) supplies a compatible element for the whole algebra.</p><p>Integrality lifts geometric points of \\(A_m\\) to \\(A\\). If a coset in \\(Z/Z_m\\) fixes a point of \\(A_m\\), compare a lift and its translate. They differ by \\(Z_m\\); freeness upstairs makes the coset trivial.</p><p>For a finite constant group \\(K\\) acting geometrically freely on an affine scheme, choose elements \\(b_1,\\ldots,b_{|K|}\\) whose evaluations on an orbit form an invertible matrix. If\n\\[\nD_b=\\det(g(b_j))_{g\\in K,j},\n\\]\nthen \\(D_b^2\\) is invariant. After inverting it, solve uniquely for the coefficients of every element in the basis \\(b_j\\); uniqueness makes the coefficients invariant. The same matrix gives the tensor torsor identity. These neighborhoods cover the invariant spectrum by integrality.</p><p>Thus \\(\\operatorname{Spec}A_m\\) is a finite faithfully flat \\(Z/Z_m\\)-torsor over \\(\\operatorname{Spec}A^Z\\). Passing to the filtered union gives faithful flatness and the torsor identity for \\(A\\). The map is affine, hence quasi-compact.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Propositions 2.4–2.6", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are equivalences \\( \\mathcal M_0[1/t]\\simeq BZ,\\qquad \\mathcal N[1/t]\\simeq\\operatorname{Spec}k. \\) The first is split by \\(v_1=1\\), \\(v_{i>1}=0\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(p&gt;5\\), \\(k=\\mathbb F_{p^2}\\), \\(q=p+1\\), and the stacks and sheaves of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\n\\Lambda=\\mu_q(k),\\quad R_d=k[x]/(x^d),\\quad R=k[[x]].\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F_0\\) be the Honda law \\(v_2=1\\), \\(v_i=0\\) for \\(i\\ne2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G^1\\) be its proconstant group of strict automorphisms, and set\n\\[\nG_{\\mathrm{up}}=G^1\\rtimes\\Lambda,\\qquad\nH=G^1/Z,\\qquad G=H\\rtimes\\Lambda.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are equivalences\n\\[\n\\mathcal M_0[1/t]\\simeq BZ,\\qquad\n\\mathcal N[1/t]\\simeq\\operatorname{Spec}k.\n\\]\nThe first is split by \\(v_1=1\\), \\(v_{i&gt;1}=0\\).</p><p>The law \\(F_x\\), with \\(v_1=x,v_2=1,v_{i&gt;2}=0\\), gives compatible presentations\n\\[\n\\mathcal M_k/(t^d)=[\\operatorname{Spec}R_d/G_{\\mathrm{up}}],\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal N/(t^d)=[\\operatorname{Spec}R_d/G],\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\mathcal X_k/(t^d)=[\\operatorname{Spec}R_d/\\Lambda].\n\\]\nSheaves are smooth semilinear representations. The coefficient action satisfies\n\\[\ng(x)\\in x+x^2R,\\qquad\n\\lambda(x)=\\lambda^{-1}x.\n\\]\nIf \\(u\\) is the chart basis of the weight line and \\(t=xu\\), then\n\\[\ng(u)=\\frac{x}{g(x)}u,\\qquad \\lambda(u)=\\lambda u.\n\\]\nEvery strict label representing \\(g\\in H\\) lifts to a formal isomorphism \\(F_{g(x)}\\to F_x\\) over \\(R\\).</p><p>The sheaves \\(T'\\) and \\(T_k\\) are \\(t\\)-monic. Their ordinary quotients\n\\[\nT'_d=T'/t^d,\\qquad T_{k,d}=T_k/t^d\n\\]\nrepresent their derived multiplication cofibers. On the chart,\n\\[\nT'_d=C_{\\mathrm{lc}}(G/\\Lambda,R_d)=C_{\\mathrm{lc}}(H,R_d)\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\n(gF)(s)=g\\bigl(F(g^{-1}s)\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe corresponding description of \\(T_{k,d}\\) uses \\(G_{\\mathrm{up}}/\\Lambda\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 3.1 and 3.2 give the open and nilpotent presentations. The chart maps on the thickenings are subgroup maps for \\(\\Lambda\\), so their pushforward is coinduction.</p><p>For a semilinear \\(G\\)-module \\(M\\), an \\(R_d\\)-linear \\(\\Lambda\\)-equivariant functional \\(\\epsilon:M\\to R_d\\) has adjoint\n\\[\nm\\longmapsto\\bigl(s\\longmapsto s(\\epsilon(s^{-1}m))\\bigr).\n\\]\nThis function is locally constant and constant on cosets; evaluation at \\(1\\Lambda\\) is the inverse adjunction. This proves the action formula.</p><p>Multiplication by \\(t\\) is injective on \\(\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal X_k}\\). Affineness and the projection formula preserve injectivity under either chart pushforward and identify the quotients with pushforwards from \\(\\mathcal X_k/(t^d)\\). Monicity identifies ordinary quotients with multiplication cofibers.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Proposition 2.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition 3, \\( \\mathcal M_0[1/t]\\simeq BZ,\\qquad \\mathcal N[1/t]\\simeq\\operatorname{Spec}k, \\) with the stated splitting.", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition 3,\n\\[\n\\mathcal M_0[1/t]\\simeq BZ,\\qquad\n\\mathcal N[1/t]\\simeq\\operatorname{Spec}k,\n\\]\nwith the stated splitting.</p>", "proof": "<p>Normalize \\(v_1=1\\) by the weight-one grading. An arrow from the splitting law to an arbitrary normalized law is obtained by imposing \\(\\eta_Rv_{j+1}=0\\) successively. Modulo preceding equations,\n\\[\n\\eta_Rv_{j+1}=t_j^p-t_j+\\text{a polynomial in }t_{&lt;j}.\n\\]\nThese equations are successively finite étale, and their limit is faithfully flat.</p><p>On the splitting law the automorphism equations reduce recursively to \\(t_j^p=t_j\\). The automorphisms \\([1+ap^j]\\) supply each prescribed next digit in \\(\\mathbb F_p\\); successive correction and completeness identify the strict stabilizer with \\(Z\\). No grading stabilizer remains because \\(v_1=1\\). Base change to \\(k\\) and quotient by \\(Z\\) give the second equivalence.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Proposition 2.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The nilpotent-chart presentations, coefficient actions, weight-line actions, and formal-isomorphism lifting assertion in Proposition 3 hold.", "statement": "<p>The nilpotent-chart presentations, coefficient actions, weight-line actions, and formal-isomorphism lifting assertion in Proposition 3 hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>On \\(V(t^d)\\), \\(v_2\\) is invertible. Normalize it to \\(1\\) by adjoining a \\(q\\)-th root of the grading parameter. The cover has coordinates\n\\[\n(v_1,v_2)=(\\lambda x,\\lambda^q).\n\\]\nOn \\(\\mathcal X_k\\) the remaining grading group is \\(\\Lambda\\).</p><p>For an arrow with left end \\(x\\), grading parameter \\(\\rho\\), and strict parameters \\(t_j\\), its right chart coordinate is \\(y=x/\\rho\\). The remaining equations are\n\\[\n\\eta_Rv_2=\\rho^q,\\qquad \\eta_Rv_{j+2}=0\\quad(j\\geq1).\n\\]\nModulo \\(x\\), the first right unit is \\(1\\), and the later equations reduce, modulo predecessors, to\n\\[\nt_j^{p^2}-t_j=0.\n\\]\nThus the special-fiber arrow scheme is proconstant with labels\n\\[\n(\\rho_0,t_1^0,t_2^0,\\ldots)\\in\\Lambda\\times k^{\\mathbb N_{&gt;0}}.\n\\]</p><p>Each prescribed label lifts uniquely through square-zero layers \\((x^b)/(x^{b+1})\\). The special-fiber Jacobian is lower triangular after earlier-row operations, with first diagonal entry a unit and all later diagonal entries \\(-1\\). Recursive solution on the infinite product gives existence and uniqueness over every chart algebra. Functoriality identifies composition with composition of special-fiber labels.</p><p>Regard a \\(g\\)-arrow as running from \\(g(x)\\) to \\(x\\). Composing on its source gives the left semilinear action. Strict labels are tangent to the identity; grading acts as stated. Central \\([z]\\) fixes \\(x\\) and grading, so quotienting gives \\(G\\).</p><p>For a strict-label lift, \\(\\rho\\in1+xR\\). There is a unique \\(\\chi\\in1+xR\\) with \\(\\chi^{1-p}=\\rho\\). The change \\(T\\mapsto\\chi T\\) sends\n\\[\nv_i\\mapsto\\chi^{1-p^i}v_i=\\rho^{m_i}v_i.\n\\]\nComposing it with the lifted strict arrow gives \\(F_{g(x)}\\to F_x\\). Finally invariance of \\(t=xu\\) gives the weight-line formula over \\(R\\), and reduction gives it over \\(R_d\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Proposition 2.8", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The ring \\(\\Delta\\) has a complete separated decreasing filtration given by ordered weights \\( \\mathrm{wt}(x)=4,\\qquad \\mathrm{wt}(U_1)=\\mathrm{wt}(U_2)=1,\\qquad \\mathrm{wt}(U_3)=3, \\) and …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the group \\(H\\) and its action on \\(R=k[[x]]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G^1(i)\\) consist of Honda automorphisms whose digits \\(t_j\\) vanish for \\(1\\leq j&lt;i\\), and let \\(H(i)\\) be its image in \\(H\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(\\Gamma_s=H(2s+1)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(g_1,g_2\\) with first digits an \\(\\mathbb F_p\\)-basis of \\(k\\), and \\(g_3=\\gamma\\) with only nonzero digit \\(t_2=\\theta\\notin\\mathbb F_p\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\n\\Omega=k[[H]],\\qquad \\Delta=R[[H]],\\qquad U_i=g_i-1.\n\\]\nThe second ring is the completed skew group ring, with coefficients on the left and \\(ga=g(a)g\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The ring \\(\\Delta\\) has a complete separated decreasing filtration given by ordered weights\n\\[\n\\mathrm{wt}(x)=4,\\qquad\n\\mathrm{wt}(U_1)=\\mathrm{wt}(U_2)=1,\\qquad\n\\mathrm{wt}(U_3)=3,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\operatorname{gr}\\Delta=k[\\sigma_x,A,B,C].\n\\]\nThe element \\(x\\) is normal,\n\\[\n\\Delta/(x)=\\Omega,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{gr}\\Omega=k[A,B,C].\n\\]\nThe group \\(H\\) has the ordered coordinates and subgroup descriptions stated in Lemma 4.1 below.</p>", "proof": "<p>At level \\(H/\\Gamma_s\\), ordered group elements and difference monomials with exponents below \\(p^s\\) are related by an invertible binomial change of basis. Thus ordered \\(U_i\\)-monomials form a topological basis of \\(\\Omega\\); adjoining powers of \\(x\\) gives one for \\(\\Delta\\).</p><p>Initially filter by closed spans of words of weight at least a prescribed integer. These stages tend to zero: in every finite quotient, \\(x\\) is normal and nilpotent, and modulo \\(x\\) the other letters generate the nilpotent augmentation ideal of a finite \\(p\\)-group algebra. Conversely the ordered basis shows the stages contain deep open neighborhoods.</p><p>Interchanging letters strictly raises weight. For \\(U_1,U_2\\), the commutator lies in \\(H(2)\\), so its difference from \\(1\\) has weight at least \\(3&gt;2\\). Commuting \\(U_1\\) or \\(U_2\\) with \\(U_3\\) gives an element of \\(\\Gamma_1\\), of weight at least \\(p&gt;4\\). Finally\n\\[\nU_ix-xU_i=(g_i(x)-x)g_i\n\\]\nhas weight at least \\(8&gt;\\mathrm{wt}(U_i)+4\\). Reorder modulo higher weight and iterate by completeness. Uniqueness of the ordered basis excludes further graded relations.</p><p>Normality follows from\n\\[\ngx=x\\frac{g(x)}xg.\n\\]\nWeighting group coefficients by \\(g(x)/x\\), or its inverse, gives inverse continuous changes of coefficients; at precision \\(x^d\\), refine the group quotient using its action modulo \\(x^{d+1}\\). Thus \\((x)\\) is two-sided and quotienting removes exactly the \\(x\\)-coordinate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.7", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition 4, the digit filtration is complete and separated, \\( H(i)/H(i+1)= \\begin{cases} (k,+),&i\\text{ odd},\\\\ (k/\\mathbb F_p,+),&i\\text{ even}, \\end{cases} \\qquad [H(i),H(j)]\\subseteq H(i+j). \\) …", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition 4, the digit filtration is complete and separated,\n\\[\nH(i)/H(i+1)=\n\\begin{cases}\n(k,+),&amp;i\\text{ odd},\\\\\n(k/\\mathbb F_p,+),&amp;i\\text{ even},\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\n[H(i),H(j)]\\subseteq H(i+j).\n\\]\nThe \\(p\\)-power map carries each graded digit to the identical digit two indices later. The map\n\\[\n\\mathbb Z_p^3\\to H,\\qquad\n(a_1,a_2,a_3)\\mapsto g_1^{a_1}g_2^{a_2}g_3^{a_3}\n\\]\ngives unique coordinates. Modulo \\(\\Gamma_s\\), coordinates are modulo \\(p^s\\); \\(\\Gamma_s\\) consists of coordinates divisible by \\(p^s\\). In \\(H(2)\\), the first two coordinates are divisible by \\(p\\). The element \\(\\gamma\\) commutes with \\(\\Lambda\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The jet composition laws make \\(G^1\\) a compact Hausdorff inverse limit of finite groups. The central subgroup is compact and closed. Nested cosets prove surjectivity onto the inverse limit of digit quotients. If \\(g\\) lies in every image \\(H(i)\\), the nonempty closed sets\n\\[\n\\{z\\in Z:gz^{-1}\\in G^1(i)\\}\n\\]\nhave nonempty intersection; separatedness upstairs then puts \\(g\\) in \\(Z\\).</p><p>An automorphism beginning in digit \\(i\\) is \\(1+h\\), where \\(h\\) has order at least \\(p^i\\). Composition orders give the commutator inclusion. In\n\\[\n(1+h)^p-1,\n\\]\nthe leading term at order \\(p^{i+2}\\) is \\(ph\\), because \\([p]_{F_0}(T)=T^{p^2}\\); for \\(p&gt;3\\), all other terms have larger order. Its digit is unchanged. The central subgroup first occurs with an \\(\\mathbb F_p\\)-digit at an even index, giving the quotients.</p><p>From level \\(s\\) to \\(s+1\\), the \\(p^s\\)-powers of \\(g_1,g_2\\) correct the two odd digits, and the \\(p^s\\)-power of \\(g_3\\) corrects the remaining even digit. Commutators enter only later steps. These corrections are unique, and completeness gives the coordinates. The weight \\(m_2=q\\) is trivial on \\(\\Lambda\\), while the other digits of \\(\\gamma\\) vanish, proving commutation.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Proposition 2.12 and the derived-category foundations of Lemmas 2.9–2.11", "depth": 0, "snippet": "These periodic categories have the stable injective models and bounded sections computations described in Lemmas 5.1–5.3.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For any stack \\(\\mathcal S\\) used above, let \\(\\mathcal D(\\mathcal S)\\) be the quasi-isomorphism localization of homological complexes of quasi-coherent sheaves with\n\\[\nC_{j+D}=C_j(1),\\qquad d:C_j\\to C_{j-1}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">A sheaf is regarded as a pure complex in residue zero, repeated with the twists.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\n\\pi_jC=[\\mathcal O[j],C]_{\\mathcal D(\\mathcal S)}.\n\\]\nThese are sections-homotopy groups, not sheaf homology.</p><p class=\"li\">Projective dimension below means projective dimension in the Ext sense.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>These periodic categories have the stable injective models and bounded sections computations described in Lemmas 5.1–5.3. In particular,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{pd}_{\\mathcal N}(\\mathcal O)\\leq4,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{pd}_{\\mathcal M_k}(\\mathcal O),\n\\operatorname{pd}_{\\mathcal M_0}(\\mathcal O)\\leq5,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{pd}_{\\mathcal M}(\\mathcal O)\\leq6.\n\\]\nThe derived vertical invariants satisfy\n\\[\nr_*M=\\ker(c-1),\\qquad\nR^1r_*M=\\operatorname{coker}(c-1),\\qquad\nR^{&gt;1}r_*M=0.\n\\]\nSections-homotopy commutes with filtered colimits and telescopes.</p>", "proof": "<p>A \\(t^\\infty\\)-torsion sheaf on \\(\\mathcal N\\) is the filtered union of sheaves killed by powers of \\(t\\). Lemmas 5.2 and 5.3 give sections-cohomology vanishing above three. The open \\(\\operatorname{Spec}k\\) has exact sections. For arbitrary \\(M\\), the kernel and cokernel of \\(M\\to M[1/t]\\) are \\(t^\\infty\\)-torsion. The two resulting short exact sequences add one degree, giving the bound four.</p><p>Vertical invariants are the kernel of \\(c-1\\). Kernel and cokernel descend by smoothness and centrality and give a two-term delta functor. Efface the cokernel by embedding into \\(l_*l^*M\\). On the arrow chart, \\(c\\) acts by translation on a \\(Z\\)-torsor. Locally split it; a locally constant function has finite central period. Increase that period by \\(p\\). The repeated values sum to zero in characteristic \\(p\\), so the function integrates as a discrete difference. Thus \\(c-1\\) is surjective on this effacement, proving the derived-invariants formula.</p><p>Since \\(r^*\\) is exact, \\(r_*\\) preserves injectives. Composing derived sections with its two-term calculation gives the bound five on \\(\\mathcal M_k\\). Finite flat scalar extension commutes with the coinduced resolution and detects vanishing, giving the same on \\(\\mathcal M_0\\).</p><p>For \\(\\mathcal M\\), \\(p\\)-power-torsion sheaves have the characteristic-\\(p\\) bound five, by finite filtrations and filtered unions. On the rational open, logarithms leave only \\(B\\mathbb G_m\\), whose sections are exact. Apply the kernel–cokernel argument to \\(M\\to M[1/p]\\), giving six.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.9", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For each flat affine groupoid stack used in the paper, twisted periodic complexes admit a stable injective model structure with quasi-isomorphisms as weak equivalences and monomorphisms as cofibrations.", "statement": "<p>For each flat affine groupoid stack used in the paper, twisted periodic complexes admit a stable injective model structure with quasi-isomorphisms as weak equivalences and monomorphisms as cofibrations. Fibrant objects have injective sheaf terms, and maps into fibrant objects are computed up to compatible chain homotopy.</p><p>If a sheaf \\(W\\) satisfies \\(\\operatorname{Ext}^{&gt;a}(W,-)=0\\) for finite \\(a\\), maps from its pure shifts are computed by applying \\(\\operatorname{Hom}(W,-)\\) termwise to a quasi-isomorphic replacement with \\(\\operatorname{Hom}(W,-)\\)-acyclic terms.</p>", "proof": "<p>The sheaf categories are Grothendieck by flat affine descent. For generators, choose a cardinal dominating the base and arrow rings. Starting from a bounded-size family of comodule elements, repeatedly adjoin components of finite coaction expressions and close under module operations. Flatness lets the coaction identities restrict to the resulting bounded-size subcomodules. These form a generating set. Disk objects from sheaf generators give generators for periodic complexes.</p><p>Apply Smith recognition in Beke’s form [8]. Quasi-isomorphisms satisfy two-out-of-three and retract closure and are accessible: close bounded-size subarrows under coactions and differentials and adjoin preimages for cone cycles. Iteration expresses every quasi-isomorphism as a suitably directed union of bounded-size quasi-isomorphic subarrows. Monomorphisms are cofibrantly generated by subobject inclusions of generators. Acyclic monomorphisms are stable under pushout and transfinite composition by AB5.</p><p>A map right orthogonal to all monomorphisms is a split epimorphism with injective kernel. An injective complex is contractible, since its inclusion into the cone of its identity splits. Hence such maps are quasi-isomorphisms. This verifies recognition.</p><p>Mapping cylinders respect twists and identify ordinary cones with homotopy cofibers. The shift is an equivalence, giving stability. Disk tests give injective terms for fibrant objects, and cylinders give the chain-homotopy calculation.</p><p>For the last assertion, the cone of a quasi-isomorphism between termwise acyclic replacements is acyclic with acyclic terms. Dimension shifting through its cycle sequences moves the obstruction to exactness past degree \\(a\\). The uniform Ext bound makes this valid for the unbounded periodic unfolding.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma 2.10", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Derived sections of sheaves on the stacks under consideration commute with filtered colimits and are computed by the flat affine coinduced resolution, compatibly with closed base change.", "statement": "<p>Derived sections of sheaves on the stacks under consideration commute with filtered colimits and are computed by the flat affine coinduced resolution, compatibly with closed base change.</p><p>If \\(\\operatorname{Ext}^{&gt;a}(\\mathcal O,-)=0\\), a finite truncation gives a sections-acyclic resolution computing sections-homotopy on periodic complexes. Consequently sections-homotopy commutes with filtered colimits and telescopes.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose an affine fpqc chart \\(l:S\\to\\mathcal S\\), with \\(S\\) affine. The functor \\(I=l_*l^*\\) is exact, its unit is monic, and its unit cokernel \\(J\\) is exact. Values of \\(I\\) are sections-acyclic by flat affine adjunction. Iteration gives the coinduced resolution.</p><p>On charts these functors and ordinary sections of coinduced terms commute with filtered colimits; AB5 gives the same for cohomology. Closed base change gives the identical base-changed resolution.</p><p>Under the dimension bound, terminate with \\(J^a\\), which is sections-acyclic by dimension shifting. The functors commute with weight twists, so finite totalization and Lemma 5.1 compute sections on periodic complexes. Colimits commute termwise.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "Lemma 2.11", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Invariants of smooth \\(k\\)-linear \\(H\\)-modules have cohomological dimension at most three.", "statement": "<p>Invariants of smooth \\(k\\)-linear \\(H\\)-modules have cohomological dimension at most three. The same is true for \\(G=H\\rtimes\\Lambda\\), and for sections on \\(\\mathcal N/(t^d)\\), for every \\(d\\geq1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lift the Koszul resolution of \\(k\\) over\n\\[\n\\operatorname{gr}\\Omega=k[A,B,C]\n\\]\nto a length-three resolution by finite free compact left \\(\\Omega\\)-modules. At each step lift homogeneous generators of the graded kernel into the actual complete kernel. Successive subtraction of initial symbols converges and gives a strict surjection. The next kernel has the next Koszul associated graded. The final graded kernel vanishes, so separatedness makes the actual kernel zero.</p><p>Continuous \\(\\operatorname{Hom}_\\Omega(-,M)\\) gives a delta functor with terms finite sums of \\(M\\). On \\(C_{\\mathrm{lc}}(H,M_{\\mathrm{vec}})\\), evaluation and translation identify it with continuous \\(k\\)-linear Hom from the compact resolution. Exact sequences of compact \\(k\\)-vector spaces split continuously by duality with discrete vector spaces, so positive cohomology vanishes. Every smooth module embeds into such a coinduction; effaceability identifies the functor with derived invariants.</p><p>For \\(G\\), take \\(H\\)-invariants and then exact \\(\\Lambda\\)-invariants. Restriction preserves injectives because finite-index induction is exact. Forgetting semilinear \\(R_d\\)-module structure also preserves injectives, having the exact free-\\(R_d\\)-module left adjoint. Proposition 3 therefore gives the bound on the thickenings.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 2.13", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The functor \\(Q\\) computes \\(Rr_*\\) and preserves quasi-isomorphisms.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Choose an affine law chart \\(l:S\\to\\mathcal M_k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Set \\(I=l_*l^*\\), and let \\(J\\) be its unit cokernel.</p><p class=\"li\">On periodic complexes put\n\\[\nQ=[r_*I\\longrightarrow r_*J]\n\\]\nin homological degrees \\(0,-1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The functor \\(Q\\) computes \\(Rr_*\\) and preserves quasi-isomorphisms. There are natural identifications\n\\[\nQT_k\\simeq T',\n\\qquad\n\\pi_*(r^*U)\\cong\\pi_*(Qr^*U)\n\\]\nand natural triangles\n\\[\nU\\longrightarrow Qr^*U\\longrightarrow U[-1]\\longrightarrow U[1]\n\\qquad(U\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathcal N)).\n\\]\nNo splitting of the final connecting map is asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>The composite \\(rl\\) is affine, so values of \\(I\\) are \\(r_*\\)-acyclic. Proposition 5 and\n\\[\n0\\to M\\to IM\\to JM\\to0\n\\]\nshow that values of \\(J\\) are also \\(r_*\\)-acyclic. Both \\(r_*I\\) and \\(r_*J\\) are exact. Their finite totalization preserves quasi-isomorphisms and agrees on injective-term fibrant replacements with derived pushforward. The weight line is pulled back, so the construction respects periodicity.</p><p>Flat affine adjunction along \\(f_k,f'\\) gives \\(Rr_*T_k\\simeq T'\\). Derived adjunction with \\(r^*\\mathcal O=\\mathcal O\\) gives the sections identification.</p><p>For a sheaf pulled back from \\(\\mathcal N\\), both vertical kernel and cokernel are that sheaf. Hence naturally\n\\[\n0\\to U\\to r_*Ir^*U\\to r_*Jr^*U\\to U\\to0.\n\\]\nApply this termwise to periodic complexes. Its connecting maps give the triangle, with homological signs.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Proposition 3.1 and Lemma 3.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Localization is flat affine open restriction followed by pushforward and embeds the open derived category fully faithfully.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work in \\(\\mathcal D(\\mathcal N)\\), \\(\\mathcal D(\\mathcal M_k)\\), or \\(\\mathcal D(\\mathcal M_0)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(d\\geq1\\), put\n\\[\nP/t^d=\\operatorname{cofib}(t^d:P[dD]\\to P),\n\\quad\n\\widehat P=\\operatorname*{holim}_dP/t^d,\n\\quad\nLP=P[1/t].\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">An object is \\(t\\)-invertible if \\(t:P[D]\\to P\\) is an equivalence, and complete if \\(P\\to\\widehat P\\) is an equivalence.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Localization is flat affine open restriction followed by pushforward and embeds the open derived category fully faithfully. Completion is exact and idempotent. Complete objects are precisely those right orthogonal in every shift to \\(t\\)-invertible objects.</p><p>The fiber of \\(P\\to\\widehat P\\) is \\(t\\)-invertible,\n\\[\nP/t^d\\simeq\\widehat P/t^d,\n\\]\nand the fracture square\n\\[\n\\begin{matrix}\nP&amp;\\longrightarrow&amp;\\widehat P\\\\\n\\downarrow&amp;&amp;\\downarrow\\\\\nLP&amp;\\longrightarrow&amp;L\\widehat P\n\\end{matrix}\n\\]\nis a homotopy pullback. Moreover\n\\[\n\\pi_iLP=\\operatorname*{colim}_{s\\geq0}\\pi_{i+sD}P.\n\\]</p><p>The open categories have the splitting and realization properties of Lemma 7.1.</p>", "proof": "<p>The localization telescope\n\\[\nP\\to P[-D]\\to P[-2D]\\to\\cdots\n\\]\nis its termwise colimit by AB5. On charts this is localization at the principal open; flat affine adjunction gives full faithfulness.</p><p>The fiber of \\(P\\to\\widehat P\\) is the limit of the tower\n\\[\n\\cdots\\xrightarrow tP[(d+1)D]\\xrightarrow tP[dD]\\xrightarrow t\\cdots.\n\\]\nShifting the tower preserves its limit, so \\(t\\) is invertible on the fiber. Multiplication by \\(t^d\\) is nullhomotopic on \\(P/t^d\\). Maps into it from a \\(t\\)-invertible object vanish in every shift, and the same holds after taking the inverse limit.</p><p>Limits are exact in the stable category. If \\(Q\\) is right orthogonal to invertible objects, the fiber of \\(Q\\to\\widehat Q\\) is both invertible and right orthogonal, hence zero. This gives idempotence and the characterization. The invertible fiber has zero \\(t^d\\)-quotients, proving the reduction equivalence. Localizing its fiber triangle gives the pullback square.</p><p>Proposition 5 permits sections to commute with the localization telescope, giving the colimit formula. Lemma 7.1 supplies the open assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The category \\(\\mathcal D(\\mathcal N[1/t])\\) is the category of \\(D\\)-periodic complexes of \\(k\\)-vector spaces.", "statement": "<p>The category \\(\\mathcal D(\\mathcal N[1/t])\\) is the category of \\(D\\)-periodic complexes of \\(k\\)-vector spaces. Every object splits into the sum over period residues of its pure homology objects, and every periodic graded homology map is realizable.</p><p>The category \\(\\mathcal D(\\mathcal M_0[1/t])\\) is the category of \\(D\\)-periodic complexes of smooth \\(Z\\)-representations over \\(\\mathbb F_p\\). Its objects likewise split into pure homology objects. Maps can have an \\(\\operatorname{Ext}^1\\)-component, but a pure object has no self-map to its \\(2\\)-shift when \\(D&gt;3\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 3 identifies the opens, and \\(t\\) trivializes the weight line. Over \\(k\\), split cycles and boundaries.</p><p>For \\(BZ\\), set \\(s=c-1\\). Smooth representations are locally \\(s\\)-nilpotent \\(\\mathbb F_p[s]\\)-modules, since\n\\[\n(1+s)^{p^a}=1+s^{p^a}.\n\\]\nThis torsion category is hereditary. Embed a torsion module in a divisible \\(\\mathbb F_p[s]\\)-module and take its \\(s\\)-power-torsion submodule. It remains divisible, hence injective; its quotient is again divisible torsion, giving an injective resolution of length one.</p><p>For a fibrant periodic complex \\(I\\), with cycles \\(Z_j\\), boundaries \\(B_j\\), and homology \\(H_j\\), heredity gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ext}^1(H_j,B_j)\n=\n\\operatorname{Ext}^2(H_j,Z_{j+1})=0.\n\\]\nThe identity of \\(H_j\\) lifts to \\(Z_j\\). Choose lifts through one period and repeat them, giving the splitting quasi-isomorphism. Periodizing a length-one injective resolution computes maps from pure objects. For \\(D&gt;3\\), neither resolution degree zero nor one contributes to a self-map of shift two.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Proposition 3.3 and Corollary 3.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is an identification of graded algebras \\( \\mathcal E_{sD}=(x^{-s}\\Delta)^\\Lambda, \\qquad \\mathcal E_n=0\\quad(n\\notin D\\mathbb Z). \\) Products are left-coefficient skew convolution.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work on \\(\\mathcal N\\), with \\(R=k[[x]]\\), \\(\\Delta=R[[H]]\\), and \\(\\Lambda=\\mu_q(k)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The element \\(x\\) is normal and regular; \\(\\Delta[x^{-1}]\\) denotes its two-sided localization.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\n\\mathcal E_n=[\\widehat T'[n],\\widehat T'].\n\\]\nProducts are composition: \\(ab\\) means apply \\(b\\), then the appropriately shifted \\(a\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nA_{\\mathrm{op}}=(\\Delta[x^{-1}])^\\Lambda.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an identification of graded algebras\n\\[\n\\mathcal E_{sD}=(x^{-s}\\Delta)^\\Lambda,\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal E_n=0\\quad(n\\notin D\\mathbb Z).\n\\]\nProducts are left-coefficient skew convolution. Every operation has a unique compatible system of ordinary sheaf-map representatives\n\\[\nT'_d\\to T'_d(s),\n\\]\nrealizing products strictly.</p><p>The degree-\\(D\\) operation \\(t\\) corresponds to \\(1\\in x^{-1}\\Delta\\) and is central in \\(\\mathcal E\\). With \\(t\\) recording degree \\(D\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal E[t^{-1}]=A_{\\mathrm{op}}[t^{\\pm1}].\n\\]\nOn invariant sections, an operation acts by\n\\[\na(f)=\\int_Ha(h)h(f).\n\\]</p><p>For every \\(j\\in\\mathbb Z\\), the objects\n\\[\n\\widehat T'[j],\\qquad \\widehat T_k[j],\\qquad\\widehat T_0[j]\n\\]\nhave no self-maps to their \\(2\\)-shifts.</p>", "proof": "<p>Normality and regularity follow from the skew relations\n\\[\nhx=x\\frac{h(x)}xh,\\qquad\nxh=\\frac{x}{h(x)}hx\n\\]\nand their inverse continuous coefficient changes.</p><p>The invertible fiber of \\(T'\\to\\widehat T'\\) makes maps into \\(\\widehat T'\\) computable from \\(T'\\). Lemma 8.1 and the Milnor sequence remove extension and derived-limit terms, producing unique compatible ordinary representatives.</p><p>Evaluation at \\(1\\Lambda\\) identifies a representative with a \\(\\Lambda\\)-equivariant \\(R_d\\)-linear functional\n\\[\nC_{\\mathrm{lc}}(H,R_d)\\to R_du^s.\n\\]\nDualizing functions on finite group quotients gives measures. Normalize the twist by\n\\[\nR_du^s\\cong x^{-s}R/x^{d-s}R,\\qquad u^s\\mapsto x^{-s}.\n\\]\nThis is equivariant because \\(g(u)=x/g(x)\\,u\\). Taking coefficient and group limits gives \\((x^{-s}\\Delta)^\\Lambda\\).</p><p>If \\(b\\) describes a map, its value at \\(h\\) on \\(F\\) is\n\\[\nh\\left(\\int_zb(z)(h^{-1}F)(z)\\right)\n=\n\\int_zh(b(z))F(hz).\n\\]\nThe projection formula is pointwise:\n\\[\nC_{\\mathrm{lc}}(H,R_d)\\otimes R_du^j\n\\to C_{\\mathrm{lc}}(H,R_du^j),\\qquad\nF\\otimes l\\mapsto(h\\mapsto F(h)l).\n\\]\nThus twisting does not change the normalized measure. Evaluation of a composite is\n\\[\n\\int_h\\int_za(h)h(b(z))F(hz),\n\\]\nthe left-coefficient skew convolution \\(ab\\). These identities hold at finite precision, including negative twists; group quotients may be refined to detect the units \\(h(x)/x\\).</p><p>On invariant sections the input is the orbit function of its value at the identity, giving the action formula. Multiplication by \\(t=xu\\) normalizes to coefficient \\(1\\). Inverting it exhausts the fractional coefficient lattices, proving the localization formula.</p><p>The self-map vanishing follows from the degree calculation for \\(\\widehat T'\\), and from Lemma 8.1 for \\(\\widehat T_k\\). Finite separable scalar extension to \\(k\\) commutes with completion by trace-duality adjunction and detects zero maps because its unit splits over \\(\\mathbb F_p\\); this gives the assertion for \\(\\widehat T_0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Finite-reduction calculation in the proof of original Proposition 3.3.", "statement": "<p>Finite-reduction calculation in the proof of original Proposition 3.3.</p><p>For every \\(d\\geq1\\),\n\\[\n[T'[sD],T'_d]\n=\n\\operatorname{Hom}(T',T'_d(s)),\n\\qquad\n[T'[n],T'_d]=0\\quad(n\\notin D\\mathbb Z).\n\\]\nThe ordinary Hom groups have surjective reduction maps in \\(d\\), and each map factors uniquely through \\(T'_d\\). The same vanishing and surjectivity hold for the full-center chart \\(T_k\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Flat affine adjunction computes maps on \\(\\mathcal X_k\\), with target \\(\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal X_k/(t^d)}\\). Work on \\([\\operatorname{Spec}k[x]/\\Lambda]\\). If \\(M\\) is the restriction of \\((f')^*T'\\), then \\(M\\) is \\(x\\)-monic and\n\\[\nM/x^dM=C_{\\mathrm{lc}}(H,R_d).\n\\]\nThis is free over \\(R_d\\), and averaging makes it equivariantly projective.</p><p>Choose an equivariant projective resolution of \\(M\\) of length one over \\(k[x]\\): the kernel of a free equivariant surjection is \\(k[x]\\)-projective because \\(k[x]\\) is hereditary, and averaging promotes equivariant projectivity. Reduction stays exact by \\(x\\)-monicity. Hom into \\(R_du^s\\) factors through the reduced resolution, whose positive cohomology vanishes.</p><p>Periodize this bounded projective resolution. Since \\(D&gt;2\\), augmentation and relation degrees do not coincide modulo \\(D\\). Only ordinary maps in degrees \\(sD\\) and their first extensions could occur; the extensions vanish.</p><p>Equivariant projectivity at level \\(d+1\\) lifts functionals through \\(R_{d+1}u^s\\to R_du^s\\), proving surjectivity. Ordinary maps kill \\(t^d\\), giving factorization. Replacing \\(H\\)-functions by \\(G_{\\mathrm{up}}/\\Lambda\\)-functions proves the full-center assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Proposition 3.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is an exact fully faithful functor \\( \\mathcal R:\\operatorname{Perf}(\\mathcal E)\\to\\mathcal D(\\mathcal N) \\) sending \\(\\mathcal E\\) to \\(\\widehat T'\\), with image \\( \\operatorname{Thick}(\\widehat T'). \\) For a …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Give \\(\\mathcal E\\) the grading of Proposition 8 and zero differential.</p><p class=\"li\">A finite-cell right dg \\(\\mathcal E\\)-module is a finite iterated extension of shifts of \\(\\mathcal E\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A perfect module is a derived retract of a finite-cell module; write \\(\\operatorname{Perf}(\\mathcal E)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an exact fully faithful functor\n\\[\n\\mathcal R:\\operatorname{Perf}(\\mathcal E)\\to\\mathcal D(\\mathcal N)\n\\]\nsending \\(\\mathcal E\\) to \\(\\widehat T'\\), with image\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Thick}(\\widehat T').\n\\]\nFor a finite-cell module \\(P\\), its image is the homotopy limit of an actual compatible tower \\(Y_d\\) whose terms are finite sums of shifts of \\(T'_d\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a homogeneous basis compatible with a cell filtration. Replace a basis vector \\(e_b\\) of degree \\(b\\) by \\(T'_d[b]\\). A degree-\\(j\\) right-module entry\n\\[\ne_b\\mapsto e_{b'}a,\\qquad a\\in\\mathcal E_{b+j-b'}\n\\]\nis represented in chain degree \\(m\\) by\n\\[\na:(T'_d)_{m-b}\\to(T'_d)_{m+j-b'}.\n\\]\nUse the unique representatives of Proposition 8, including differential entries \\(j=-1\\).</p><p>Strict composition makes \\(d^2=0\\) an equality of actual maps. All coefficient degrees are even. The Hom differential\n\\[\nd_Qf-(-1)^jfd_P\n\\]\nis respected, as are chain maps, homotopies, signed shifts, and cones. All choices are compatible in \\(d\\).</p><p>The multiplication order agrees with right modules: an endomorphism determined by \\(1\\mapsto a\\) is left multiplication, and composing \\(b\\) then \\(a\\) gives \\(ab\\).</p><p>Take homotopy limits. Exactness of limits makes levelwise cones into realized cones. Basis changes give tower isomorphisms. Finite cells are homotopically projective by induction on their free-cell filtrations, so homotopy maps compute derived maps.</p><p>On the generator and all shifts, full faithfulness is Proposition 8. Induct first on target cells, then source cells using long exact mapping sequences. Extend to retracts using idempotent completeness. The image is precisely ordinary thick closure.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Proposition 3.6 and Lemma 3.7", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For \\(Y=\\mathcal R(P)\\), \\(P\\in\\operatorname{Perf}(\\mathcal E)\\), \\( \\pi_*Y=H_*(P\\otimes_{\\mathcal E}^{\\mathbb L}\\mathcal L), \\qquad W(Y)=H_*(P\\otimes_{\\mathcal E}^{\\mathbb L}\\Phi[t^{\\pm1}]). \\) For finite-cell models …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Define the graded left \\(\\mathcal E\\)-module\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{sD}=(x^{-s}R)^\\Lambda,\\qquad\n\\mathcal L_n=0\\quad(n\\notin D\\mathbb Z),\n\\]\nwith zero differential and the action of Proposition 8.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\n(\\mathcal L_d)_{sD}\n=(x^{-s}R/x^{d-s}R)^\\Lambda,\n\\qquad\n\\Phi=k((x))^\\Lambda=k((x^q)).\n\\]\nThen \\(\\mathcal L=\\varprojlim_d\\mathcal L_d\\) and\n\\[\n\\mathcal L[t^{-1}]=\\Phi[t^{\\pm1}].\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a complete object \\(Y\\), write \\(W(Y)=\\pi_*LY\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(Y=\\mathcal R(P)\\), \\(P\\in\\operatorname{Perf}(\\mathcal E)\\),\n\\[\n\\pi_*Y=H_*(P\\otimes_{\\mathcal E}^{\\mathbb L}\\mathcal L),\n\\qquad\nW(Y)=H_*(P\\otimes_{\\mathcal E}^{\\mathbb L}\\Phi[t^{\\pm1}]).\n\\]\nFor finite-cell models, ordinary cellular tensors compute these derived tensors.</p><p>For a finite-cell realization \\(Y=\\operatorname*{holim}_eY_e\\),\n\\[\nY/t^d\\simeq Y_d\n\\]\nnaturally, and its projection is intrinsic reduction. Thus \\(\\pi_iY\\) has the intrinsic profinite topology from these finite reduction groups.</p><p>For a retract \\(Y'\\) defined by an idempotent, let \\(e_d\\) be the induced derived idempotent of \\(Y_d\\). Then \\(Y'/t^d\\) is its split summand and\n\\[\n\\pi_iY'=\\varprojlim_d\\operatorname{im}(\\pi_ie_d)\n\\]\nwith its intrinsic profinite topology.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a finite cell, Lemma 10.1 allows inverse limits through the exact sequences defining cycles, boundaries, and homology. The Milnor sequence and finite graded freeness give\n\\[\n\\pi_*Y\n=\nH_*\\left(\\varprojlim_dP\\otimes_{\\mathcal E}\\mathcal L_d\\right)\n=\nH_*(P\\otimes_{\\mathcal E}\\mathcal L).\n\\]\nThe tensor–limit interchange is degreewise finite. A finite-cell module is homotopically flat by its cell filtration, so the ordinary tensor computes the derived one.</p><p>Take the \\(t\\)-colimit and use Proposition 7 and exactness of filtered colimits to obtain the overlap formula. Naturality permits passage to perfect retracts. Lemma 10.2 identifies the reductions and topologies.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Finite-level calculation in original Proposition 3.6.", "statement": "<p>Finite-level calculation in original Proposition 3.6.</p><p>For a finite-cell module \\(P\\) and its realization tower \\(Y_d\\),\n\\[\n\\pi_*Y_d=H_*(P\\otimes_{\\mathcal E}\\mathcal L_d).\n\\]\nIn each degree, the terms, cycles, boundaries, and homology of these section complexes are finite groups. Their inverse systems satisfy the Mittag–Leffler condition.</p>", "proof": "<p>Terms of \\(Y_d\\) are finite sums of shifts of \\(T'_d\\). Their twists are sections-acyclic by affine adjunction to the nilpotent chart, where \\(\\Lambda\\)-invariants are exact. Proposition 5 therefore computes sections by the termwise section complex, which is exactly \\(P\\otimes_{\\mathcal E}\\mathcal L_d\\), including its differential and maps.</p><p>The field \\(k\\) is finite, each fractional lattice modulo \\(x^d\\) is finite-dimensional, and only finitely many cells occur. All the displayed groups are finite, so their inverse systems are Mittag–Leffler.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.7", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The intrinsic-reduction and retract assertions in Proposition 10 hold.", "statement": "<p>The intrinsic-reduction and retract assertions in Proposition 10 hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>Multiplication by \\(t^d\\) is strictly zero on \\(Y_d\\). For \\(e\\geq d\\), map the ordinary cone of\n\\[\nt^d:Y_e[dD]\\to Y_e\n\\]\nto \\(Y_d\\) by the transition on the unshifted summand and zero on the shifted summand. These are compatible natural chain maps. Taking limits gives \\(Y/t^d\\to Y_d\\).</p><p>On the free generator it is the equivalence\n\\[\n\\widehat T'/t^d\\simeq T'/t^d=T'_d\n\\]\nof Proposition 7, represented by ordinary quotient. Compatibility with cones and shifts proves the result by cell induction.</p><p>Lemma 10.1 and the Milnor sequence give\n\\[\n\\pi_iY=\\varprojlim_d\\pi_iY_d.\n\\]\nNaturality sends a retract idempotent to \\(e_d\\); reduction preserves the retract. On finite groups, compatible image–kernel decompositions show that taking images commutes with inverse limits. This gives the retract formula and intrinsic topology.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Proposition 3.8", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every \\(P\\in\\operatorname{Thick}(T')\\) has finite \\(\\pi_iP\\) for every integer \\(i\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathcal N)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use completion, localization, and \\(W_i(\\widehat P)=\\pi_iL\\widehat P\\) as above.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every \\(P\\in\\operatorname{Thick}(T')\\) has finite \\(\\pi_iP\\) for every integer \\(i\\).</p><p>More generally, if every \\(\\pi_iP\\) is finite, then \\(\\pi_i\\widehat P\\) is naturally profinite from its intrinsic finite reductions. The space \\(W_i(\\widehat P)\\) has a locally compact Hausdorff \\(k\\)-vector-space topology as the increasing union of the compact open images of\n\\[\n\\pi_{i+sD}\\widehat P,\\qquad s\\geq0.\n\\]\nThe image of \\(\\pi_iLP\\) is discrete and cocompact.</p><p>If \\(Q\\) has the same finiteness property, every map \\(\\widehat P\\to\\widehat Q\\) induces continuous maps on complete and overlap homotopy, whether or not it comes from a global map.</p><p>For a perfect realization of \\(\\widehat P\\), these topologies agree with Proposition 10.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(T'\\), affine adjunction reduces sections to twists on the weighted projective line\n\\[\n\\mathcal X_k=\\mathbb P_k(1,q).\n\\]\nThe two-variable principal-open Čech complex has, in each fixed weight, finitely many nonnegative monomials in \\(H^0\\) and finitely many Laurent monomials with both exponents negative in \\(H^1\\). Thus every sections-homotopy group is finite. This property is thick.</p><p>For arbitrary degreewise finite \\(P\\), multiplication cofibers have finite homotopy, and the Milnor sequence gives\n\\[\n\\pi_i\\widehat P=\\varprojlim_d\\pi_i(P/t^d)\n\\]\nwith zero first derived limit. The reduction equivalence of Proposition 7 identifies this with intrinsic reduction topology.</p><p>The fracture long exact sequence has\n\\[\n\\pi_i\\widehat P\\oplus\\pi_iLP\\to W_i(\\widehat P)\n\\]\nwith finite kernel and cokernel. Put \\(C_s=\\pi_{i+sD}\\widehat P\\), and let \\(K_s\\) be its overlap image. Each \\(C_s\\to K_s\\) has finite kernel. Give \\(K_s\\) its compact quotient topology. Multiplication maps are continuous and have finite cokernel, so\n\\[\nK_s\\subseteq K_{s+1}\n\\]\nis a finite-index closed and open embedding. Their union is the overlap, yielding its locally compact topology.</p><p>The open image \\(B\\) meets \\(K_0\\) in a finite group. A neighborhood avoiding its nonzero points shows \\(B\\) is discrete and hence closed. The quotient by \\(K_0+B\\) is finite, so the quotient by \\(B\\) is compact.</p><p>Any complete map induces maps on intrinsic finite reductions, hence continuous maps on inverse limits and on each compact overlap stage. Proposition 10 identifies the perfect-realization topology, compatibly with \\(t\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Lemmas 4.1 and 4.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "If \\(g\\in H(i)\\setminus H(i+1)\\), then \\( \\ell(g)\\leq r_i:=\\sum_{j=1}^ip^{\\lfloor j/2\\rfloor}. \\) The action is faithful, \\( \\kappa:=\\sum_{l\\geq1}\\frac1{N_l}\\leq\\frac p{p-1}, \\) and …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(H\\), its digit filtration, \\(R=k[[x]]\\), and \\(\\Delta=R[[H]]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nK_0=k((x)),\\qquad w=x^{-q},\\qquad\\Phi=K_0^\\Lambda=k((w^{-1})).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(g\\in H\\), define\n\\[\nz_g=\\frac{g(x)}x,\\qquad h_g=\\frac{g(w)}w=z_g^{-q},\n\\qquad u_g=h_g-1,\n\\qquad \\ell(g)=\\operatorname{ord}_x(u_g).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nH_l=\\{g:\\ell(g)\\geq l\\},\\qquad N_l=[H:H_l].\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(g\\in H(i)\\setminus H(i+1)\\), then\n\\[\n\\ell(g)\\leq r_i:=\\sum_{j=1}^ip^{\\lfloor j/2\\rfloor}.\n\\]\nThe action is faithful,\n\\[\n\\kappa:=\\sum_{l\\geq1}\\frac1{N_l}\\leq\\frac p{p-1},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\Gamma_s\\subseteq H_{p^s}\\subseteq H(2s-1),\n\\qquad\nN_{p^s}\\geq p^{3(s-1)}.\n\\]</p><p>Choose a balanced sequence \\(b_j^H\\in H\\), meaning that modulo \\(H_l\\) its indices enumerate cosets repeatedly, with equality of cosets exactly for congruent indices modulo \\(N_l\\). Set\n\\[\nu_j=u_{b_j^H},\n\\qquad\nr'_m=\\sum_{l\\geq1}\\left\\lfloor\\frac m{N_l}\\right\\rfloor\\leq\\kappa m.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nD_m=\n\\sum_{i=0}^m\n\\frac{x^{r'_m}}{\\prod_{0\\leq j\\leq m,\\ j\\ne i}(u_i-u_j)}\n\\,\\delta_{b_i^H}\n\\in\\Delta.\n\\]\nIts moments are\n\\[\n\\int_HD_m(g)u_g^n=\n\\begin{cases}\n0,&amp;0\\leq n&lt;m,\\\\\nx^{r'_m},&amp;n=m,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nand belong to \\(x^{r'_m+n-m}R\\) for \\(n&gt;m\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 12.1 proves the group bounds. Balanced enumerations exist by refinement of nested finite quotients and compactness. Faithfulness makes \\(N_l\\to\\infty\\), so the sums \\(r'_m\\) are finite and the nodes are distinct.</p><p>For fixed \\(i\\leq m\\), at most \\(\\lfloor m/N_l\\rfloor\\) other indices are congruent to \\(i\\) modulo \\(N_l\\). The orbit distance formula bounds the denominator valuation by \\(r'_m\\), proving integrality.</p><p>Lagrange interpolation identifies the divided difference of \\(Z^n\\) with the coefficient of \\(Z^m\\) in its remainder modulo \\(\\prod_{i=0}^m(Z-u_i)\\). This is zero for \\(n&lt;m\\), one for \\(n=m\\), and a homogeneous polynomial of degree \\(n-m\\) in the \\(u_i\\) for \\(n&gt;m\\). Since \\(u_i\\in xR\\), the moment estimates follow.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The displacement, faithfulness, summability, and subgroup bounds in Proposition 12 hold.", "statement": "<p>The displacement, faithfulness, summability, and subgroup bounds in Proposition 12 hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a strict representative whose first nonidentity digit is \\(b\\ne0\\) at index \\(i\\); for even \\(i\\), choose \\(b\\notin\\mathbb F_p\\). Proposition 3 gives\n\\[\n\\widetilde g:F_y\\to F_x,\\qquad y=g(x),\\qquad\n[p]_{F_x}(T)=xT^p+_{F_x}T^{p^2},\n\\]\nwhose reduction differs from the identity first by \\(bT^{p^i}\\).</p><p>Extend \\(v(x)=1\\) to a completed algebraic closure of \\(K_0\\). Weierstrass preparation for \\([p^d]_{F_x}\\) gives \\(p^d\\) distinct torsion points, each of multiplicity \\(p^d\\). They form a cyclic group: the subgroup killed by \\(p\\) has order \\(p\\). A primitive level-\\(d\\) point \\(\\zeta\\) has\n\\[\nv(\\zeta)=\\lambda_d=\\frac1{(p-1)p^{2d-1}}.\n\\]\nFor \\(d=1\\), equate the two \\(p\\)-series valuations; induction uses dominance of the \\(p^2\\)-power term below \\(\\lambda_1\\).</p><p>For large \\(d\\), the residue of\n\\[\n\\frac{\\widetilde g(\\zeta)-_{F_x}\\zeta}{\\zeta^{p^i}}\n\\]\nis \\(b\\). For \\(\\tau-\\!_{F_x}\\zeta=[a]_{F_x}\\zeta\\), with \\(s=\\nu_p(a)\\), its valuation is \\(p^{2s}\\lambda_d\\). At \\(2s=i\\), its normalized residue lies in \\(\\mathbb F_p^\\times\\), so cannot cancel \\(b\\). Therefore\n\\[\nv(\\widetilde g(\\zeta)-\\tau)=p^{\\min(i,2s)}\\lambda_d.\n\\]\nCounting the \\((1-1/p)p^{d-s}\\) multiples of exact parameter \\(s\\), with multiplicities, gives\n\\[\nv([p^d]_{F_x}(\\widetilde g(\\zeta)))\n=\n\\frac{p^{\\lceil i/2\\rceil}+p^{\\lfloor i/2\\rfloor+1}-1}{p-1}\n=\nr_i+\\frac p{p-1}.\n\\]\nBut \\(F_y\\equiv F_x\\pmod{x^{\\ell(g)+1}}\\), so the same value is strictly greater than \\(\\ell(g)+1\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\ell(g)&lt;r_i+\\frac1{p-1},\n\\]\nproving the bound. If \\(y=x\\), the value would be zero, proving faithfulness.</p><p>For \\(r_i&lt;l\\leq r_{i+1}\\),\n\\[\nN_l\\geq[H:H(i+1)]=p^{2i-\\lfloor i/2\\rfloor}.\n\\]\nThe interval has length \\(p^{\\lfloor(i+1)/2\\rfloor}\\), so contributes at most \\(p^{-i}\\) to \\(\\kappa\\).</p><p>The inequality \\(r_{2s-2}&lt;p^s\\) gives \\(H_{p^s}\\subseteq H(2s-1)\\) and its index bound. Every tangent operator \\(g-1\\) raises valuation at least one; in characteristic \\(p\\),\n\\[\ng^{p^s}-1=(g-1)^{p^s}.\n\\]\nApply this to the ordered coordinates of \\(\\Gamma_s\\), giving the other inclusion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Proposition 4.6", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is \\(a\\in\\mathcal E_0\\) with \\( a\\bmod x=U_3,\\qquad \\sigma(a)=C, \\) whose lower-going matrix on \\(\\Phi\\) satisfies:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Choose an odd integer \\(M\\) such that\n\\[\nM\\geq81,\\qquad p&gt;16M^2,\\qquad 2\\lfloor M/8\\rfloor&gt;p^{1/3}.\n\\]\nThese conditions hold for every sufficiently large prime, by taking \\(M\\sim p^{2/5}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(w=x^{-q}\\), \\(\\Phi=k((w^{-1}))\\), and \\(\\mathcal E_0=\\Delta^\\Lambda\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\n\\alpha=-\\frac12\\in\\mathbb Z_p,\\qquad \\delta=\\frac{p-1}2,\n\\]\n\\[\nd_i=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{(i+1/2)p}{M}\\right\\rfloor,\\quad\ns_0=\\frac{M-1}2,\\quad R_0=\\lfloor M/8\\rfloor,\n\\]\n\\[\nI=\\{s_0-R_0+1,\\ldots,s_0+R_0\\},\\quad\nB_0=2R_0,\\quad\n\\beta=\\log_pB_0&gt;\\frac13.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">A lower-going matrix has entries \\(A_{l,j}=0\\) for \\(l&gt;j\\). Its matching is defined by the rank differences of Lemma 13.3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(a\\in\\mathcal E_0\\) with\n\\[\na\\bmod x=U_3,\\qquad \\sigma(a)=C,\n\\]\nwhose lower-going matrix on \\(\\Phi\\) satisfies:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. For every \\(J\\in\\mathbb Z\\), only finitely many matched pairs \\((l,j)\\) cross \\(J\\), meaning \\(l\\leq J&lt;j\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. There are \\(c&gt;0,N_0\\) such that for every \\(N\\geq N_0\\), at least \\(cN^\\beta\\) columns in \\([-N,0]\\) and at least \\(cN^\\beta\\) rows in \\((0,N]\\) remain forever unmatched.</p>", "proof": "<p>Weighting measures is well-defined at finite coefficient and group levels: for precision \\(x^e\\), choose \\(p^s\\geq e\\); Proposition 12 gives \\(\\Gamma_s\\subset H_e\\), making \\(h_g^z\\bmod x^e\\) constant on these cosets. The operator is continuous and preserves poles.</p><p>At depth \\(b\\), with \\(X=p^r\\leq qb&lt;pX\\), rank tests are periodic in the column label modulo \\(pX\\). For each fixed lower label, Lemma 13.1 permits prescribing no pivot at any chosen set of at most \\(M\\) eligible next digits, and pivots at all other eligible digits. Indeed interpolation imposes at most \\(M\\) values on degree-\\(M\\) polynomials. Each unwanted extra equality is a proper affine hyperplane over \\(k\\), containing fraction \\(1/p^2\\); at most \\(p\\) such hyperplanes cannot cover the solution space.</p><p>Start with Lemma 13.2 and depth \\(d=\\lfloor p/M\\rfloor\\). All smaller depths vanish because \\(qd&lt;p^2\\). Match every column except labels \\(z=\\alpha+j\\) with\n\\[\nz_0=\\delta,\\qquad z_1\\in\\{d_0,\\ldots,d_{M-1}\\}.\n\\]\nSurviving rows have \\(z_0=\\delta-d\\) and the same next digits. No second eligible depth congruent to \\(d\\bmod p\\) occurs before \\(qb=p^2\\).</p><p>Inductively, at \\(X=p^r\\), \\(r\\geq2\\), the surviving labels modulo \\(X\\) are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|ccc}\n&amp;z_0&amp;z_j,\\ 1\\leq j\\leq r-2&amp;z_{r-1}\\\\ \\hline\n\\text{columns}&amp;\\delta&amp;d(I)&amp;d_i,\\ 0\\leq i&lt;M\\\\\n\\text{rows}&amp;\\delta-d&amp;d(I-1)&amp;d_i,\\ 0\\leq i&lt;M.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nEach lower label has all \\(p\\) new-digit replicas. Cluster by \\(i\\). The first possible band pairs column cluster \\(i\\) with row cluster \\(i-1\\), cyclically, at depths\n\\[\nX(1/M-2/p)\\leq b\\leq X(1/M+2/p),\n\\qquad b\\geq X/(2M).\n\\]\nThe lower portions differ by at most \\((p/4+3)X/p^2\\). Same-cluster differences and cyclic complements lie outside the stage’s allowed interval \\([X/q,X-X/q)\\); the prime bounds separate the adjacent band from later bands.</p><p>Process the band by depth. A lower label is full if all next-digit replicas remain. At an edge with full endpoints, match all replicas, except for column cluster \\(i\\in I\\), where retain the \\(M\\) center digits \\(d_0,\\ldots,d_{M-1}\\). There is no wrap for retained clusters, so row and column retained digits agree. If either endpoint is nonfull, there are at most \\(M\\) eligible replicas; prescribe no pivots.</p><p>Each pair of adjacent clusters is a complete bipartite graph with equally many lower labels, \\(B_0^{r-2}\\). If full labels survived on either side, equal removals would leave full labels on both, whose edge would already have matched them. Thus all are paired once. Later depths admit no pivots. Exactly clusters \\(I\\) and \\(I-1\\) survive, with all center digits at the new place, proving the induction. Lemma 13.1 gives convergence.</p><p>Fix \\(J\\). At large \\(X\\), a matched pair crossing \\(J\\) would have\n\\[\nJ&lt;j\\leq J+X(1/M+2/p).\n\\]\nIts lower label is \\((X-1)/2+j\\), without wrap, and its next digit is \\(\\delta\\). Eligibility places its cluster within three places of \\(s_0\\), hence in \\(I\\). But that next digit was retained, excluding a match. Only finitely many earlier depths cross \\(J\\).</p><p>Finally take admissible column or row labels eventually equal to the digits of \\(\\alpha\\). They give genuine integer offsets \\(j\\). At the highest modified place \\(r\\), choose a digit below \\(\\delta\\) for columns or above \\(\\delta\\) for rows; lower changes cannot reverse its sign. The \\(r-1\\) intermediate digits give at least \\(B_0^{r-1}\\) offsets of absolute value below \\(p^{r+1}\\). If \\(p^{r+1}\\leq N&lt;p^{r+2}\\),\n\\[\nB_0^{r-1}\\geq p^{-3\\beta}N^\\beta.\n\\]\nThis gives both uniform defect counts.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Represent \\(a\\) by weighting a measure \\(\\nu\\in\\Delta^\\Lambda\\) by \\(h_g^\\alpha\\), so \\( a(w^j)=w^j\\int_H\\nu(g)h_g^{\\alpha+j}. \\) For depth \\(b=j-l\\), define \\( f_b(z)=[x^{qb}]\\int_H\\nu(g)h_g^z. \\) If …", "statement": "<p>Represent \\(a\\) by weighting a measure \\(\\nu\\in\\Delta^\\Lambda\\) by \\(h_g^\\alpha\\), so\n\\[\na(w^j)=w^j\\int_H\\nu(g)h_g^{\\alpha+j}.\n\\]\nFor depth \\(b=j-l\\), define\n\\[\nf_b(z)=[x^{qb}]\\int_H\\nu(g)h_g^z.\n\\]\nIf \\(X=p^r\\leq qb&lt;pX\\) and \\(b\\geq X/(2M)\\), adding a measure in \\((x^{X+1}\\Delta)^\\Lambda\\) changes \\(f_b\\) by an arbitrary polynomial of degree at most \\(M\\) in the digit \\(z_r\\), independently for every lower label \\(z\\bmod X\\), without changing smaller-depth coefficients.</p><p>Corrections in increasing depths converge in \\(\\Delta\\) and preserve reduction modulo \\(x\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(0\\leq m&lt;(M+1)X\\), use scalar multiples of \\(x^{qb-r'_m}D_m\\), averaged under \\(\\Lambda\\). Proposition 12 gives\n\\[\nqb-r'_m\n\\geq\n\\left(\\frac q{2M}-\\kappa(M+1)\\right)X&gt;X.\n\\]\nThe correction belongs to \\(x^{X+1}\\Delta\\). Expanding \\(h_g^z=(1+u_g)^z\\), the moment formulas show that it vanishes below order \\(qb\\) and changes order \\(qb\\) by \\(\\binom zm\\bmod p\\). Averaging preserves this coefficient because \\(x^{qb}\\) is invariant and\n\\[\nh_{\\lambda g\\lambda^{-1}}=\\lambda(h_g).\n\\]</p><p>The characteristic-\\(p\\) identity\n\\[\n(1+v)^z=\\prod_{i\\geq0}(1+v^{p^i})^{z_i}\n\\]\nexpresses the binomial functions as digit-binomial products. The lower-digit bases span all functions; the indices \\(0,\\ldots,M\\) in digit \\(r\\) span polynomials of degree at most \\(M&lt;p\\). All products occur in the stated range of \\(m\\).</p><p>Each stage has finitely many depths and labels, and its correction lies in \\(x^{X+1}\\Delta\\). At fixed coefficient precision, all sufficiently late stages vanish in every compatible finite group quotient. Thus the corrections converge. Smaller-depth vanishing separately stabilizes each finite matrix block.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is an initial \\(\\nu\\in\\Delta^\\Lambda\\), reducing to \\(\\delta_\\gamma-\\delta_1\\), with \\( \\int_H\\nu(g)u_g^n=0\\qquad(0\\leq n<p^2). \\) Consequently \\( \\int_H\\nu(g)h_g^z\\in x^{p^2}R \\qquad(z\\in\\mathbb Z_p). \\) After …", "statement": "<p>There is an initial \\(\\nu\\in\\Delta^\\Lambda\\), reducing to \\(\\delta_\\gamma-\\delta_1\\), with\n\\[\n\\int_H\\nu(g)u_g^n=0\\qquad(0\\leq n&lt;p^2).\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n\\int_H\\nu(g)h_g^z\\in x^{p^2}R\n\\qquad(z\\in\\mathbb Z_p).\n\\]\nAfter corrections from Lemma 13.1, the weighted operator still satisfies\n\\[\na\\bmod x=U_3,\\qquad \\sigma(a)=C.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 12 gives \\(N_2\\geq p^2\\). Distinct classes modulo \\(H_2\\) have distinct residues of \\(u_g/x\\) in \\(k\\), so \\(N_2\\leq|k|=p^2\\). The first \\(p^2\\) balanced nodes divided by \\(x\\) therefore have distinct residues.</p><p>Since \\(\\gamma\\) commutes with \\(\\Lambda\\), \\(h_\\gamma\\) is invariant and its positive exponents are divisible by \\(q&gt;1\\). Thus \\(u_\\gamma/x\\) has zero residue. The measure \\(\\delta_\\gamma-\\delta_1\\) integrates all \\((u/x)^n\\), \\(n&lt;p^2\\), to zero modulo \\(x\\). The Vandermonde matrix at the first \\(p^2\\) nodes is invertible over \\(R\\); solve for corrections with coefficients in \\(xR\\) killing the moments exactly, then average.</p><p>Expansion gives the \\(x^{p^2}\\)-bound. Later corrections and weighting by \\(h_g^\\alpha\\equiv1\\pmod x\\) preserve reduction. Since \\(x\\) has weight four and \\(U_3\\) weight three, the initial symbol remains \\(C\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.3", "orig": "Lemma 4.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a lower-going matrix, let \\(R(l,j)\\) be its rank on the finite block \\([l,j]\\), and put \\(R(l,j)=0\\) if \\(l>j\\).", "statement": "<p>For a lower-going matrix, let \\(R(l,j)\\) be its rank on the finite block \\([l,j]\\), and put \\(R(l,j)=0\\) if \\(l&gt;j\\). Then\n\\[\nR(l,j)-R(l,j-1)-R(l+1,j)+R(l+1,j-1)\n\\]\nis zero or one and defines a partial matching of rows and columns. The rank counts matched pairs in the block.</p><p>When entries are fixed by increasing depth, a new match at \\((l,j)\\) is possible exactly when both endpoints remain unmatched and the corner avoids one exceptional scalar determined by smaller depths. Decisions at a fixed depth are independent.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(d_j(l)=R(l,j)-R(l,j-1)\\). It is zero or one. Column independence on rows at least \\(l+1\\) remains independence after adding row \\(l\\), since the newly admitted column \\(l\\) is zero on old rows. Thus \\(d_j(l)\\geq d_j(l+1)\\), with at most one jump per column. The row argument gives at most one per row; telescoping counts matches.</p><p>Both corner endpoints are unmatched exactly when\n\\[\nR(l,j-1)=R(l+1,j-1)=R(l+1,j).\n\\]\nAfter moving the corner column first, write the block as\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}c&amp;r\\\\u&amp;B\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThe rank equalities give \\(r=\\rho B\\), \\(u=B\\eta\\). Row and column operations reduce the new contribution to \\(c-\\rho B\\eta\\), independently of the choices of \\(\\rho,\\eta\\). The unique exceptional scalar is \\(\\rho B\\eta\\). All other entries have smaller depth.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "Lemma 5.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "A column \\(j\\) is unmatched exactly when \\(\\mathcal K\\) contains a vector with leading term \\(w^j\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(a\\) be the operator of Proposition 13.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nF_J\\Phi=w^Jk[[w^{-1}]],\\qquad\n\\mathcal Q=\\Phi/a\\Phi,\\qquad\n\\mathcal K=\\ker(a:\\Phi\\to\\Phi).\n\\]\nGive \\(\\mathcal K\\) its subspace topology and \\(\\mathcal Q\\) its quotient topology.</p><p class=\"li\">The leading position of a nonzero Laurent series is its greatest exponent of \\(w\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A column \\(j\\) is unmatched exactly when \\(\\mathcal K\\) contains a vector with leading term \\(w^j\\). A row \\(l\\) is matched exactly when it is the leading position of a nonzero image vector.</p><p>For every \\(J\\),\n\\[\n\\dim_k\\frac{a\\Phi\\cap F_J\\Phi}{a(F_J\\Phi)}\n=\n\\#\\{(l,j)\\text{ matched}:l\\leq J&lt;j\\}&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nThe image \\(a\\Phi\\) is closed. Both \\(\\mathcal Q\\) and \\(\\mathcal K\\) are Hausdorff locally compact, with compact open subspaces respectively the images of \\(F_J\\Phi\\) and \\(\\mathcal K\\cap F_J\\Phi\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>If column \\(j\\) is unmatched, for every \\(L\\leq j\\) the finite equations have a solution in\n\\[\nS_L=\\{f\\in w^j+F_{j-1}\\Phi:a(f)\\in F_{L-1}\\Phi\\}.\n\\]\nThese are nonempty nested closed subsets of a compact space. Their intersection supplies a kernel vector. Conversely such a vector makes the column dependent in every finite block. Thus\n\\[\n\\dim_k a(F_j\\Phi)/a(F_{j-1}\\Phi)\n\\]\nis one for a matched column and zero otherwise.</p><p>A matched row is realized by a finite column combination with that leading row. Conversely, if \\(a(f)\\) has leading position \\(l\\), split \\(f\\) into its finite part in rows at least \\(l\\) and its lower tail. Triangularity makes the tail irrelevant to those rows, exhibiting the row increment in a finite block.</p><p>For \\(J'&gt;J\\), the image quotient has dimension equal to the number of matched columns in \\((J,J']\\). Projection to \\(F_{J'}\\Phi/F_J\\Phi\\) has rank \\(R(J+1,J')\\). Its kernel therefore counts precisely pairs \\(l\\leq J&lt;j\\leq J'\\). Taking the union over \\(J'\\) gives the formula.</p><p>The space \\(a(F_J\\Phi)\\) is compact. The displayed quotient is finite, so \\(a\\Phi\\cap F_J\\Phi\\) is a finite union of compact translates and is closed. The \\(F_J\\Phi\\) form an open cover, proving image closedness. The remaining topological assertions follow from openness of the quotient map and closedness of the kernel.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "Lemma 5.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "In every degree, compatibly with \\(t\\)-periodicity, \\( W_i(Y)=\\mathcal Q\\oplus\\mathcal K. \\) The map \\(\\pi_iY\\to W_i(Y)\\) has finite kernel and compact open image, commensurable with \\(C_0^{\\mathrm{lat}}\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let\n\\[\nm=\\operatorname{cofib}(a:\\widehat T'\\to\\widehat T'),\n\\qquad\nY=\\bigoplus_{r=0}^{D-1}m[r].\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(\\mathcal Q,\\mathcal K,F_J\\Phi\\) from Proposition 14, and put\n\\[\nC_0^{\\mathrm{lat}}\n=\\operatorname{im}_{\\mathcal Q}(F_0\\Phi)\n\\oplus(\\mathcal K\\cap F_0\\Phi).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>In every degree, compatibly with \\(t\\)-periodicity,\n\\[\nW_i(Y)=\\mathcal Q\\oplus\\mathcal K.\n\\]\nThe map \\(\\pi_iY\\to W_i(Y)\\) has finite kernel and compact open image, commensurable with \\(C_0^{\\mathrm{lat}}\\).</p><p>The complete topology is intrinsic from finite reductions; the overlap topology is the displayed locally compact topology. Every fixed compact stage is contained in the image of one sufficiently late \\(t\\)-translated group \\(\\pi_{i+sD}Y\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The overlap of \\(\\widehat T'\\) is supported in degrees divisible by \\(D\\), with normalized coefficient \\(\\Phi\\). Since \\(D&gt;2\\), each cone has distinct cokernel and kernel residues. In degree \\(i\\), the cokernel comes from \\(m[r]\\), \\(i\\equiv r\\pmod D\\), by the cofiber inclusion; the kernel comes from \\(m[r']\\), \\(i\\equiv r'+1\\pmod D\\), by the boundary. Their sum gives the decomposition.</p><p>For complete homotopy, Proposition 10 replaces \\(\\Phi\\) with\n\\[\n(x^{-s}R)^\\Lambda=F_{\\lfloor s/q\\rfloor}\\Phi.\n\\]\nOn a cokernel component, the kernel of its map to \\(\\mathcal Q\\) is\n\\[\n(a\\Phi\\cap F_J\\Phi)/a(F_J\\Phi),\n\\]\nfinite by Proposition 14. Its image is compact open. On a kernel component the stage is \\(\\mathcal K\\cap F_J\\Phi\\), which injects. Compact open subspaces are commensurable.</p><p>Proposition 10 identifies these calculations as inverse limits of finite reductions. Mittag–Leffler exactness gives their intrinsic compact topology; the \\(t\\)-colimit gives the stated overlap topology. The coefficient stages increase without bound, giving the final assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "Proposition 5.4 and Lemma 5.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\pi_iU\\text{ is finite for every }i,\\qquad \\widehat U\\simeq Y\\in\\operatorname{Thick}(\\widehat T'), \\qquad LU\\simeq V. \\) The structure map \\(LU\\to L\\widehat U\\) is the chosen lattice map.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(Y\\), \\(\\mathcal Q\\oplus\\mathcal K\\), and \\(C_0^{\\mathrm{lat}}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For each unmatched positive row \\(j\\), let \\(q_j=[w^j]\\in\\mathcal Q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For each unmatched nonpositive column \\(l\\), choose \\(k_l\\in\\mathcal K\\) with leading term \\(w^l\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Pair the lists in increasing \\(j\\) and increasing \\(|l|\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In each period residue, extend the paired classes modulo \\(C_0^{\\mathrm{lat}}\\) to a basis of the quotient, lift this basis retaining the pairs, and let \\(\\mathcal L_i\\) be its span.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V\\) be the open periodic object with homotopy \\(\\mathcal L_i\\), mapped to \\(LY\\) by these inclusions.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\nU=Y\\times_{LY}V\n\\]\nas a homotopy pullback.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\pi_iU\\text{ is finite for every }i,\\qquad\n\\widehat U\\simeq Y\\in\\operatorname{Thick}(\\widehat T'),\n\\qquad LU\\simeq V.\n\\]\nThe structure map \\(LU\\to L\\widehat U\\) is the chosen lattice map.</p><p>For every global map \\(g:U\\to P\\), \\(P\\in\\operatorname{Thick}(T')\\), the induced map\n\\[\nW_i(\\widehat g):\\mathcal Q\\oplus\\mathcal K\\to W_i(\\widehat P)\n\\]\nis continuous and sends every paired vector \\((q_j,k_l)\\) into the discrete lattice\n\\[\n\\operatorname{im}(\\pi_iLP\\to W_i(\\widehat P)).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The chosen span \\(\\mathcal L_i\\) projects isomorphically to the quotient by \\(C_0^{\\mathrm{lat}}\\). It is therefore a discrete closed complement, and the compact subspace surjects onto the quotient by \\(\\mathcal L_i\\), proving cocompactness. Proposition 7 realizes the lattice map as \\(V\\to LY\\).</p><p>Let \\(C_i\\) be the compact open image of \\(\\pi_iY\\). It is commensurable with \\(C_0^{\\mathrm{lat}}\\). For\n\\[\n\\pi_iY\\oplus\\pi_iV\\to\\pi_iLY,\n\\]\nthe kernel, modulo the finite kernel of \\(\\pi_iY\\to C_i\\), is \\(C_i\\cap\\mathcal L_i\\), which injects into the finite quotient \\(C_i/(C_i\\cap C_0^{\\mathrm{lat}})\\). The cokernel is a quotient of \\(C_0^{\\mathrm{lat}}/(C_i\\cap C_0^{\\mathrm{lat}})\\). The pullback exact sequence makes every \\(\\pi_iU\\) finite.</p><p>Completion kills \\(V\\) and \\(LY\\), so \\(\\widehat U\\simeq Y\\). Localization gives \\(LU\\simeq V\\), preserving the structure map.</p><p>For the final assertion, Proposition 11 applies to \\(U\\) and \\(P\\), giving continuity. Naturality of fracture sends each paired representative in \\(\\pi_iLU=\\mathcal L_i\\) through \\(\\pi_iLP\\), hence into its discrete image. An individual pair need not lift to \\(\\pi_iU\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The paired vectors are independent modulo \\(C_0^{\\mathrm{lat}}\\).", "statement": "<p>The paired vectors are independent modulo \\(C_0^{\\mathrm{lat}}\\). For all sufficiently large \\(N\\), at least \\(cN^\\beta\\) pairs satisfy simultaneously\n\\[\nj\\leq N,\\qquad |l|\\leq N.\n\\]\nTheir kernel coordinates are independent modulo \\(x^{qN+1}R\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>A nontrivial relation among the \\(q_j\\) modulo the compact image would, after subtracting an element of \\(F_0\\Phi\\), be an image of \\(a\\) whose leading row is unmatched. Proposition 14 excludes this.</p><p>Let \\(n_+(N)\\) and \\(n_-(N)\\) count the respective rows and columns. Ordered pairing gives exactly \\(\\min(n_+(N),n_-(N))\\) simultaneous pairs. Proposition 13 bounds this below by \\(cN^\\beta\\).</p><p>Their kernel leading terms are distinct\n\\[\nw^l=x^{q|l|},\\qquad q|l|\\leq qN.\n\\]\nThe lowest \\(x\\)-power of any nonzero combination therefore survives modulo \\(x^{qN+1}R\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 17", "orig": "Theorem 6.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( U_0\\notin\\operatorname{Thick}_{\\mathcal C}(T_0). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S_0\\) be a domain with principal left and right ideals.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n\\[\n\\mathcal B_0=S_0[t^{\\pm1}]\n\\]\nhave zero differential, with \\(t\\) central of positive even homological degree \\(D_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal C\\) be idempotent-complete triangulated and\n\\[\n\\Psi:\\mathcal C\\to\\operatorname{Perf}(\\mathcal B_0)\n\\]\nexact, with \\(\\Psi(T_0)\\simeq\\mathcal B_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a perfect right module \\(M\\), put\n\\[\nM^*=\\mathbb R\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal B_0}(M,\\mathcal B_0).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">A right \\(S_0\\)-module is torsion if each element is right-annihilated by a nonzero element.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(H(\\Psi(U_0))\\) is torsion degreewise and nonzero.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume that for every \\(P\\in\\operatorname{Thick}_{\\mathcal C}(T_0)\\) and \\(g:U_0\\to P\\),\n\\[\nH(\\Psi(g))=0\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nH((\\Psi(g))^*)=0.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nU_0\\notin\\operatorname{Thick}_{\\mathcal C}(T_0).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose \\(U_0\\) belongs to the thick closure. Choose a retract insertion \\(g_0:U_0\\to P_0\\) and a tower from Lemma 17.2. Let \\(g_j:U_0\\to P_j\\) be its composites. Work backward from \\(P_s=0\\), proving both ordinary- and dual-homology vanishing.</p><p>At one extended step write\n\\[\nF\\to P_1'\\to P_2'\\to F[1],\n\\qquad\ng_1':\\Psi(U_0)\\to P_1',\\quad g_2':\\Psi(U_0)\\to P_2'.\n\\]\nBy Lemma 17.1,\n\\[\nP_2'\\simeq V'\\oplus L'\n\\]\nwith \\(V'\\) finite free shifts and \\(H(L')\\) torsion. Projection components to \\(V'\\) are dual-homology classes. Dual vanishing of \\(g_2'\\) makes its composite to \\(V'\\) zero as a derived map. Therefore \\(g_1'\\) lifts to\n\\[\nZ'=\\operatorname{fib}(P_1'\\to V'),\n\\]\nwhich fits into\n\\[\nF\\to Z'\\to L'\\to F[1].\n\\]</p><p>The homology map preceding \\(H(F)\\to H(Z')\\) has torsion source and free target, hence is zero. Thus \\(H(F)\\) injects. Ordinary vanishing of \\(g_2'\\) makes the lifted homology factor through this free submodule. Its source is torsion, so the factor is zero. Hence \\(H(g_1')=0\\). The assumed implication, applied to the actual tower-tail map in \\(\\mathcal C\\), restores dual vanishing.</p><p>At \\(j=0\\), \\(\\Psi(g_0)\\) is zero on homology but has a left inverse. This contradicts nonzero \\(H(\\Psi(U_0))\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 17.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every finitely generated right \\(S_0\\)-module is a direct sum of a torsion module and a finite free module and has a finite free resolution of length at most one.", "statement": "<p>Every finitely generated right \\(S_0\\)-module is a direct sum of a torsion module and a finite free module and has a finite free resolution of length at most one.</p><p>Every perfect right dg \\(S_0[t^{\\pm1}]\\)-module splits into the \\(D_0\\)-periodizations of its shifted homology modules. In particular, it is a finite sum of free shifts and a perfect module with torsion homology.</p>", "proof": "<p>Principal one-sided ideals make \\(S_0\\) noetherian on both sides. Nonzero right ideals \\(dS_0,eS_0\\) intersect: otherwise \\(e^idS_0\\), \\(i\\geq0\\), form an infinite internal direct sum, contradicting noetherianity. Cancel the least \\(e\\)-power in a finite relation to verify independence. The left argument gives the two-sided Ore division ring \\(Q_0\\).</p><p>For finite \\(M\\), the kernel of \\(M\\to M\\otimes Q_0\\) is precisely its torsion. Embed its torsion-free quotient in a finite-dimensional \\(Q_0\\)-space, then clear common left denominators coordinatewise to embed it in \\(S_0^r\\). Every submodule of \\(S_0^r\\) is finite free: project to the first coordinate, whose image is a principal ideal free of rank one or zero; split it and induct. The torsion-free quotient is therefore free, so the torsion sequence splits. A finite presentation has finite free kernel, giving the resolution.</p><p>View a dg module over \\(\\mathcal B_0\\) as a \\(D_0\\)-periodic complex. Perfect homology is finitely generated in each residue. Resolve each \\(H_i\\) by\n\\[\n0\\to R_{1,i}\\to R_{0,i}\\to H_i\\to0.\n\\]\nPlace these in degrees \\(i+1,i\\). Lift generators to cycles and relations to boundaries in a representative complex. Periodize with \\(t\\); the positive even degree respects signs. Summing the \\(D_0\\) maps is a quasi-isomorphism. Splitting homology into torsion and free parts gives the conclusion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 17.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For an idempotent-complete triangulated category \\(\\mathcal C\\), every \\(P\\in\\operatorname{Thick}_{\\mathcal C}(T_0)\\) is a retract of an object \\(P_0\\) with a finite tower …", "statement": "<p>For an idempotent-complete triangulated category \\(\\mathcal C\\), every \\(P\\in\\operatorname{Thick}_{\\mathcal C}(T_0)\\) is a retract of an object \\(P_0\\) with a finite tower\n\\[\nF_j\\to P_j\\to P_{j+1}\\to F_j[1],\n\\qquad 0\\leq j&lt;s,\\qquad P_s=0,\n\\]\nwhere each \\(F_j\\) is a finite sum of arbitrary integer shifts of \\(T_0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Finite extensions of free layers are closed under shifts and sums. Octahedra concatenate towers: for a triangle \\(A\\to E\\to B\\) and first layer \\(F_0\\to A\\to A_1\\), the cofiber \\(E_1\\) of \\(F_0\\to E\\) fits into\n\\[\nF_0\\to E\\to E_1,\\qquad A_1\\to E_1\\to B.\n\\]\nInduct on the tower of \\(A\\), then use that of \\(B\\). In particular cones have towers.</p><p>Move retracts to the end. Write finite extensions\n\\[\n\\overline A\\simeq A\\oplus A',\\qquad\n\\overline B\\simeq B\\oplus B'.\n\\]\nExtend \\(f:A\\to B\\) by zero. Its cone is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{cofib}(f)\\oplus B'\\oplus A'[1].\n\\]\nThus cones of retracts are retracts of finite extensions. The resulting class is thick and contains \\(T_0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 18", "orig": "Lemmas 6.4–6.5 and coefficient extension (6.4)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The coefficient extension \\( \\mathcal E\\to A_{\\mathrm{op}}[t^{\\pm1}] \\to\\Delta[x^{-1}][t^{\\pm1}] \\to\\mathcal B \\) is flat on both sides.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the weight-filtered ring\n\\[\n\\operatorname{gr}\\Delta=k[\\sigma_x,A,B,C].\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Microlocalize at the homogeneous prime \\((C)\\), as in Lemma 18.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) be the resulting filtered ring and set\n\\[\n\\mathcal B=S[t^{\\pm1}],\n\\]\nwhere \\(t\\) is central of homological degree \\(D\\), while \\(S\\) is in degree zero.</p><p class=\"li\">Retain the operator \\(a\\) with \\(\\sigma(a)=C\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The coefficient extension\n\\[\n\\mathcal E\\to A_{\\mathrm{op}}[t^{\\pm1}]\n\\to\\Delta[x^{-1}][t^{\\pm1}]\n\\to\\mathcal B\n\\]\nis flat on both sides.</p><p>The ring \\(S\\) is a domain with principal left and right ideals and a two-sided Ore division ring of fractions. The element \\(a\\) has no one-sided inverse in \\(S\\), and\n\\[\nS/aS\\ne0\n\\]\nis a torsion right \\(S\\)-module.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Lemma 18.1 to \\(\\Delta\\) and \\(c=C\\). The symbol of \\(x\\) is invertible in \\(\\operatorname{gr}S\\); lifting an inverse and correcting in increasing filtration makes \\(x\\) invertible in \\(S\\). Flatness therefore also holds over \\(\\Delta[x^{-1}]\\).</p><p>The tame character decomposition makes \\(\\Delta[x^{-1}]\\) finite free over \\(A_{\\mathrm{op}}\\) on either side: the unit \\(x\\) has faithful \\(\\Lambda\\)-character, and its powers identify every character space with the invariant space. Central localization at \\(t\\) is flat. This proves the displayed two-sided flat extension. Lemma 18.2 gives the remaining assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 18.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(A\\) be a complete separated filtered algebra over a finite field \\(k\\), with \\(F^nA=A\\) for \\(n\\leq0\\), and \\( \\operatorname{gr}A=k[z_1,\\ldots,z_d,c] \\) with positive generator degrees.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(A\\) be a complete separated filtered algebra over a finite field \\(k\\), with \\(F^nA=A\\) for \\(n\\leq0\\), and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{gr}A=k[z_1,\\ldots,z_d,c]\n\\]\nwith positive generator degrees. There is a filtered algebra \\(S\\) and \\(A\\to S\\) such that:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{gr}S\n\\]\nis the homogeneous localization inverting all homogeneous elements outside \\((c)\\); its exhaustive, possibly negatively indexed filtration is complete and separated; and \\(S\\) is flat as a left and right \\(A\\)-module.</p><p>For every finite good filtered right module \\(M\\), its microlocalization is \\(M\\otimes_AS\\), with associated graded the same localization of \\(\\operatorname{gr}M\\). The analogous assertion holds for left modules.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a submodule of a finite shifted filtered free module, lift homogeneous generators of its associated graded. Successive subtraction raises filtration, and completeness sums the coefficients, giving finite generation and a strict surjection. Each quotient \\(A/F^nA\\) is finite, so \\(A\\) and finite products are compact. The submodule is the compact image of a finite free module, hence closed. Thus \\(A\\) is noetherian on both sides, finite modules have complete separated good filtrations, induced and quotient filtrations are good, and two good filtrations differ by bounded shifts.</p><p>Form\n\\[\n\\widetilde A=\\bigoplus_nF^nAs^{-n},\n\\qquad\n\\widetilde M=\\bigoplus_nF^nMs^{-n},\n\\]\nwith central \\(s\\) of Rees degree \\(-1\\). Modulo \\(s^j\\), invert homogeneous elements whose reductions lie outside \\((c)\\). They are regular by induction through \\(s\\)-layers. They satisfy two-sided Ore conditions: commutators are divisible by \\(s\\), so \\(\\operatorname{ad}_d^j(e)=0\\pmod{s^j}\\); expansion gives a right factor \\(d\\) in \\(d^je\\) and a left factor \\(d\\) in \\(ed^j\\). Localization is exact by common denominators.</p><p>Let \\(M_j^\\mu\\) be the localized truncated Rees module. Define\n\\[\nF^n\\mu(M)=\\varprojlim_j(M_j^\\mu)_n,\n\\qquad\n\\mu(M)=\\bigcup_{n\\in\\mathbb Z}F^n\\mu(M).\n\\]\nRegularity of \\(s\\), followed by exact localization, gives\n\\[\n0\\to(M_j^\\mu)_{n+l}\\xrightarrow{s^l}\n(M_{j+l}^\\mu)_n\\to(M_l^\\mu)_n\\to0.\n\\]\nTransitions are surjective. Taking limits gives the inclusions and\n\\[\nF^n\\mu(M)/F^{n+l}\\mu(M)=(M_l^\\mu)_n.\n\\]\nHence completeness, separatedness, and the associated-graded formula follow.</p><p>Products give \\(S=\\mu(A)\\). Bounded-shift maps make the construction functorial and independent of good filtration. For a short exact sequence, use induced and quotient filtrations. Its Rees sequence stays exact modulo \\(s^j\\), then under Ore localization, surjective inverse limits, and filtered unions. Thus \\(\\mu\\) is exact on finite modules.</p><p>The map \\(M\\otimes_AS\\to\\mu(M)\\) is an isomorphism on free modules and hence by finite presentations. Exactness on finite modules over a noetherian ring proves left flatness; the left-module construction proves right flatness.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 18.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the microlocalization of \\(\\Delta\\) at \\((C)\\), \\(S\\) has the domain, principal-ideal, Ore, and operator properties stated in Proposition 18.", "statement": "<p>For the microlocalization of \\(\\Delta\\) at \\((C)\\), \\(S\\) has the domain, principal-ideal, Ore, and operator properties stated in Proposition 18.</p>", "proof": "<p>The graded ring is a domain, so multiplicative initial symbols make \\(S\\) a domain. Every nonzero homogeneous element is a power of \\(C\\) times a homogeneous unit.</p><p>In a nonzero right ideal choose \\(d_0\\) whose initial symbol has minimal nonnegative \\(C\\)-order. For any \\(d\\), subtract a right multiple of \\(d_0\\) matching its symbol. Repeat in increasing filtration. The multipliers converge, expressing \\(d\\) as a right multiple of \\(d_0\\). The left proof is identical. The ideal-intersection argument of Lemma 17.1 gives the Ore fractions.</p><p>The initial symbol of \\(a\\) is \\(C\\), not invertible in \\(\\operatorname{gr}S\\). A one-sided inverse would contradict this, so \\(S/aS\\ne0\\). For nonzero \\(b\\), a nonzero element in \\(bS\\cap aS\\) gives \\(bd\\in aS\\) with \\(d\\ne0\\). Every quotient class is therefore torsion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 19", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The category \\(\\mathcal C_{\\mathrm{comp}}\\) is full, thick, and idempotent-complete.", "statement": "<p>The construction following original Lemma 6.5 and equation (6.5).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_{\\mathrm{comp}}\n=\\{P\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathcal N):\n\\widehat P\\in\\operatorname{Thick}(\\widehat T')\\}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define \\(\\Psi\\) by completion, the inverse of Proposition 9, and derived scalar extension to \\(\\mathcal B=S[t^{\\pm1}]\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The category \\(\\mathcal C_{\\mathrm{comp}}\\) is full, thick, and idempotent-complete. The functor\n\\[\n\\Psi:\\mathcal C_{\\mathrm{comp}}\\to\\operatorname{Perf}(\\mathcal B)\n\\]\nis exact and sends \\(T'\\) to \\(\\mathcal B\\). It contains the glued object \\(U\\), and\n\\[\nH_i(\\Psi(U))\\cong S/aS\n\\]\nas right \\(S\\)-modules for every \\(i\\). These homology modules are nonzero torsion.</p>", "proof": "<p>Exactness and retract preservation of completion make the inverse image of the complete thick subcategory thick and idempotent-complete. Proposition 16 places \\(U\\) in it. The composite functor is exact.</p><p>On a free right module, \\(1\\mapsto a\\) is left multiplication by \\(a\\), injective because \\(S\\) is a domain, with cokernel \\(S/aS\\). The complete source \\(Y\\) is the sum of all \\(D\\) shifts of this cone. Thus each homology residue is \\(S/aS\\), nonzero torsion by Proposition 18.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 20", "orig": "Lemma 6.6", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is \\(c_0\\), depending on the fixed data, such that the image of \\( \\{f\\in x^{-L_0}R:a_1(f)=b_1(f)=0\\} \\to K_0/x^nR \\) has dimension at most \\(c_0(1+n)^{1/3}\\) for every \\(n\\geq1\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For \\(b\\in\\Delta\\), let \\(\\overline b\\) denote reduction in \\(\\Omega=\\Delta/(x)\\), and \\(\\sigma(\\overline b)\\) its subsequent weight initial symbol.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(a_1,b_1\\in\\Delta\\) satisfy\n\\[\n(\\sigma(\\overline a_1))=(C)\n\\quad\\text{in }k[A,B,C],\n\\qquad\n\\sigma(\\overline b_1)\\notin(C).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(L_0\\geq0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(c_0\\), depending on the fixed data, such that the image of\n\\[\n\\{f\\in x^{-L_0}R:a_1(f)=b_1(f)=0\\}\n\\to K_0/x^nR\n\\]\nhas dimension at most \\(c_0(1+n)^{1/3}\\) for every \\(n\\geq1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(f_1,\\ldots,f_v\\) in the joint kernel independent modulo \\(x^nR\\), and form orbit functions \\(\\varphi_i(h)=h(f_i)\\). Put \\(F_i(y)=y^{L_0}f_i(y)\\in k[[y]]\\).</p><p>For coefficients \\(c_i\\in K_0\\) of minimum valuation zero, the reduction of \\(\\sum_i c_iF_i(y)\\) is nonzero of order \\(d&lt;n+L_0\\). Weierstrass preparation gives a unit times a monic degree-\\(d\\) polynomial. Evaluating at \\(h(x)=xz_h\\) preserves polynomial valuation. Lagrange interpolation at the first \\(d+1\\) balanced orbit points expresses its leading coefficient \\(x^d\\) in terms of values with denominator valuations at most \\(r'_d\\). Some value therefore has valuation at most \\(d+r'_d=O(n)\\).</p><p>Thus the orbit functions are \\(K_0\\)-independent, and coefficients of integral-valued combinations are bounded below by \\(-O(n)\\). Their integral-valued lattice \\(\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{orb}}\\) is finite free of rank \\(v\\); its reduction injects into functions \\(H\\to k\\), since a function valued in \\(xR\\) is divisible by \\(x\\) within that lattice.</p><p>Multiplication by \\(z_h^{L_0}\\equiv1\\pmod x\\) leaves residues unchanged. The coefficient bound truncates the expression to an integral-valued polynomial in \\(z_h\\) of degree \\(J=O(n)\\). Such polynomials have normalized Newton basis\n\\[\nx^{-r'_m}\\prod_{i=0}^{m-1}(z-z_{b_i^H}),\n\\qquad 0\\leq m\\leq J.\n\\]\nBalancedness makes these integral and gives unit diagonal values at \\(z_{b_m^H}\\), proving the basis by triangular interpolation.</p><p>If two orbit points agree modulo \\(x^l\\) and \\(N_l&gt;J\\), all basis residues agree. In the product difference, replacing one factor loses at most one match per level below \\(l\\), while the replaced-factor difference contributes valuation at least \\(l\\). After normalization the difference lies in \\(xR\\).</p><p>Choose \\(s\\) with \\(N_{p^s}&gt;J\\) and \\(p^s=O(n^{1/3})\\), using Proposition 12. The residue functions factor through \\(H/\\Gamma_s\\).</p><p>For \\(\\mu=a_1,b_1\\), applying \\(h\\) to \\(\\mu(f_i)=0\\) gives, for every lattice function,\n\\[\n\\int_Hh(\\mu(g))\\varphi(hg)=0.\n\\]\nOn reduction these functions annihilate left translates of \\(\\overline a_1,\\overline b_1\\). Their dimension is bounded by\n\\[\n\\dim_k\n\\frac{\\Omega}\n{\\Omega\\overline a_1+\\Omega\\overline b_1+\n\\ker(\\Omega\\to k[H/\\Gamma_s])}.\n\\]\nIts associated graded kills at least\n\\[\nC,\\quad\\sigma(\\overline b_1),\\quad\nA^{p^s},B^{p^s},C^{p^s}.\n\\]\nModulo \\(C\\), choose a leading monomial \\(A^{u_0}B^{v_0}\\) of the nonzero polynomial \\(\\sigma(\\overline b_1)\\bmod C\\). At most \\((u_0+v_0)p^s\\) monomials with exponents below \\(p^s\\) avoid divisibility; a nonzero constant gives zero quotient. The dimension is \\(O(p^s)=O(n^{1/3})\\). Since the lattice reduction has dimension \\(v\\), this proves the claim.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 21", "orig": "Lemma 6.7", "depth": 0, "snippet": "If a right ideal \\(J\\subseteq\\Delta\\) contains \\(a\\Delta\\), is right \\(x\\)-saturated, and satisfies \\(JS=S\\), then it contains \\(b\\) with \\( \\sigma(\\overline b)\\notin(C). \\) If \\(b_0\\in\\Delta[x^{-1}]\\setminus Sa\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(a\\in\\Delta\\) with \\(\\overline a=U_3\\) and \\(\\sigma(\\overline a)=C\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the microlocalization \\(S\\) of Proposition 18.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. If a right ideal \\(J\\subseteq\\Delta\\) contains \\(a\\Delta\\), is right \\(x\\)-saturated, and satisfies \\(JS=S\\), then it contains \\(b\\) with\n\\[\n\\sigma(\\overline b)\\notin(C).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(b_0\\in\\Delta[x^{-1}]\\setminus Sa\\) annihilates a \\(k\\)-subspace\n\\[\nV\\subseteq\\ker(a:K_0\\to K_0),\n\\]\nthen some \\(b_1\\in\\Delta\\) annihilates \\(V\\) and has\n\\[\n\\sigma(\\overline b_1)\\notin(C).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For the first assertion, if every \\(j\\in J\\) reduces into \\(\\overline a\\Omega\\), subtract a right multiple of \\(a\\) and write\n\\[\nj=ad_0+j_1x.\n\\]\nRight saturation puts \\(j_1\\in J\\). Iteration gives\n\\[\nj=a(d_0+d_1x+\\cdots+d_{r-1}x^{r-1})+j_rx^r.\n\\]\nThe coefficient series converges and the remainder tends to zero, so \\(J=a\\Delta\\), contradicting \\(JS=S\\). Choose a reduction outside \\(\\overline a\\Omega\\). Repeatedly subtract right multiples matching \\(C\\)-divisible initial symbols. An infinite process would express the reduction in \\(\\overline a\\Omega\\), so it terminates outside \\((C)\\). Lift the finite subtractions.</p><p>For the second assertion, multiply \\(b_0\\) on the left by a power of \\(x\\), obtaining integral \\(b\\notin Sa\\) still annihilating \\(V\\). Whenever \\(\\overline b\\in\\Omega\\overline a\\), subtract a left multiple of \\(a\\) and divide on the left by \\(x\\). Infinite iteration would give\n\\[\nb=(d_0+xd_1+\\cdots+x^{r-1}d_{r-1})a+x^rb_r\n\\]\nand hence \\(b\\in\\Delta a\\), a contradiction. Once the reduction lies outside \\(\\Omega\\overline a\\), initial-symbol subtraction terminates outside \\((C)\\). All operations preserve annihilation of \\(V\\). Neither expansion commutes \\(x\\) through \\(a\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 22", "orig": "Proposition 6.9", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( H(\\widehat g_{\\mathcal B})=0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad H((\\widehat g_{\\mathcal B})^*)=0. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U\\) be the glued object of Proposition 16.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(g:U\\to P\\) be a global map with \\(P\\in\\operatorname{Thick}(T')\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\widehat g_{\\mathcal B}=\\Psi(g)\\) under Proposition 19.</p><p class=\"li\">Duals are module duals over \\(\\mathcal B\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nH(\\widehat g_{\\mathcal B})=0\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nH((\\widehat g_{\\mathcal B})^*)=0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Assume ordinary homology vanishes. For each residue \\(r\\), compose the cofiber inclusion with \\(\\widehat g\\):\n\\[\nh:\\widehat T'[r]\\xrightarrow{\\iota_r}m[r]\\to Y\\to\\widehat P.\n\\]\nExtend first to \\(A_{\\mathrm{loc}}=\\Delta[x^{-1}]\\), not yet to \\(S\\). Let \\(J_h\\subseteq\\Delta\\) be the right annihilator of this homology class. It contains \\(a\\Delta\\) and is right \\(x\\)-saturated.</p><p>If \\(I_h\\) is the annihilator over \\(A_{\\mathrm{loc}}\\), then\n\\[\nI_h=J_hA_{\\mathrm{loc}}.\n\\]\nThe cyclic map \\(A_{\\mathrm{loc}}/I_h\\to H_r(\\widehat P_{A_{\\mathrm{loc}}[t^{\\pm1}]})\\) is injective. Left flatness of \\(S\\) preserves this injection and commutes with homology. Its generator becomes zero because \\(\\widehat g_{\\mathcal B}\\) is a homology ghost. Thus \\(J_hS=S\\). Proposition 21 gives\n\\[\nb\\in J_h,\\qquad \\sigma(\\overline b)\\notin(C).\n\\]</p><p>Tensor the pre-microlocal relations \\(ha=hb=0\\) derivatively with \\(K_0[t^{\\pm1}]\\). They are zero derived maps out of free shifts, so no flatness of \\(K_0\\) is needed. If \\(P_{\\mathcal E}\\) represents \\(\\widehat P\\), associativity identifies the tensor complex with\n\\[\nP_{\\mathcal E}\\otimes_{\\mathcal E}^{\\mathbb L}K_0[t^{\\pm1}].\n\\]\nThe tame average\n\\[\n\\operatorname{pr}:K_0\\to\\Phi,\\qquad\nf\\mapsto|\\Lambda|^{-1}\\sum_{\\lambda\\in\\Lambda}\\lambda(f)\n\\]\nis \\(\\mathcal E\\)-linear. Projecting the derived relations gives\n\\[\nW(h)(\\operatorname{pr}(a(f)))\n=\nW(h)(\\operatorname{pr}(b(f)))=0.\n\\]</p><p>Fix a compact open target subspace \\(C_P\\). Continuous functionals killing it pull back along \\(W(h)\\operatorname{pr}\\) to \\(\\operatorname{CT}_x(uf)\\), with all \\(u\\) in one fixed \\(x^{-L_0}R\\), by continuity from Proposition 11. The displayed relations and Lemma 22.1 give\n\\[\na^\\dagger(u)=b^\\dagger(u)=0.\n\\]\nProposition 20 applies. Consequently, modulo \\(C_P\\), the selected polar images \\(W(h)(w^j)\\), \\(1\\leq j\\leq N\\), have rank \\(O(N^{1/3})\\): independent functionals on their span pull back to series independent modulo \\(x^{qN+1}R\\).</p><p>Enlarge \\(C_P\\) to contain the images of all kernel coordinates \\(k_l\\), possible because they lie in a compact source subspace. Proposition 16 places paired images in the discrete target lattice. Lemma 16.1 gives at least \\(cN^\\beta\\) simultaneous pairs. Their images modulo \\(C_P\\) have rank \\(O(N^{1/3})\\), and the lattice meets \\(C_P\\) in fixed finite dimension. Thus the paired span contains a subspace killed by \\(W(\\widehat g)\\) of dimension\n\\[\ncN^\\beta-O(N^{1/3}).\n\\]\nIts kernel projection has the same dimension even modulo \\(x^{qN+1}R\\), and lies in \\(\\mathcal K\\cap R\\).</p><p>Now take any pre-extension map\n\\[\n\\theta:\\widehat P\\to\\widehat T'[\\ell].\n\\]\nOn \\(m[r]\\), its composite with the cofiber inclusion has coefficient \\(c\\) satisfying \\(ca=0\\). The operation ring embeds in the domain \\(\\Delta[x^{-1}][t^{\\pm1}]\\), so \\(c=0\\). The composite factors as\n\\[\nm[r]\\xrightarrow{\\partial_r}\\widehat T'[r+1]\n\\xrightarrow{b'}\\widehat T'[\\ell].\n\\]\nIts cokernel-overlap component vanishes; its kernel component is \\(b'\\). Therefore \\(b'\\) annihilates all the large projected subspaces above. If its operation degree is not divisible by \\(D\\), it is already zero. Otherwise normalize by \\(t\\) and regard \\(b'\\in(\\Delta[x^{-1}])^\\Lambda\\).</p><p>If \\(b'\\notin Sa\\), Proposition 21 supplies an integral second annihilator with symbol outside \\((C)\\). Proposition 20 would bound the independent projected subspaces by \\(O(N^{1/3})\\), contradicting \\(\\beta&gt;1/3\\). Hence \\(b'\\in Sa\\). After extension it kills the connecting map because \\(a[1]\\partial_r=0\\). Thus every \\(\\theta\\widehat g\\) vanishes after extension.</p><p>For perfect \\(M\\), evaluation gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal B\\otimes_{\\mathcal E}^{\\mathbb L}M^*\n\\simeq(M_{\\mathcal B})^*,\n\\]\nfirst on free shifts and then by thick closure. Right flatness gives\n\\[\nH((M_{\\mathcal B})^*)\\cong\n\\mathcal B\\otimes_{\\mathcal E}H(M^*).\n\\]\nEvery class is a finite sum of extended pre-extension maps \\(\\theta\\), all of whose composites vanish. Naturality proves dual-homology vanishing.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 22.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.8", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every \\(b\\in\\Delta\\) has an adjoint \\(b^\\dagger\\in\\Delta\\) for \\( \\langle u,f\\rangle=\\operatorname{CT}_x(uf),\\qquad u,f\\in K_0. \\) Modulo \\(x\\), adjoint induces group inversion on \\(\\Omega\\).", "statement": "<p>Every \\(b\\in\\Delta\\) has an adjoint \\(b^\\dagger\\in\\Delta\\) for\n\\[\n\\langle u,f\\rangle=\\operatorname{CT}_x(uf),\\qquad u,f\\in K_0.\n\\]\nModulo \\(x\\), adjoint induces group inversion on \\(\\Omega\\). It preserves the conditions that an initial symbol generate \\((C)\\) or lie outside \\((C)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The pairing identifies \\(K_0\\) with its continuous \\(k\\)-linear dual: a continuous functional kills some \\(x^jR\\), and its monomial values are the reversed coefficients of a Laurent series bounded below.</p><p>Formal residue substitution in \\(dx/x\\) gives\n\\[\n(c_0\\delta_g)^\\dagger\n=\ng^{-1}(c_0)\\,\n\\frac{x(g^{-1}(x))'}{g^{-1}(x)}\n\\,\\delta_{g^{-1}}.\n\\]\nThe Jacobian is an integral unit congruent to one modulo \\(x\\). Residue substitution holds in characteristic \\(p\\) because its monomial identities involve finitely many coefficients, hold integrally in characteristic zero, and specialize.</p><p>At each coefficient precision, coefficient substitution and the Jacobian require only a finite group-action level. Integrating the formula over compatible finite quotients gives the adjoint measure. Modulo \\(x\\), it is inversion. On associated graded it sends \\(A,B,C\\) to their negatives, preserving the stated conditions.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 23", "orig": "Theorem 6.10", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For the object \\(U\\) of Proposition 16, \\( U\\notin\\operatorname{Thick}_{\\mathcal D(\\mathcal N)}(T'). \\)", "statement": "<p>For the object \\(U\\) of Proposition 16,\n\\[\nU\\notin\\operatorname{Thick}_{\\mathcal D(\\mathcal N)}(T').\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Proposition 17 with\n\\[\nS_0=S,\\quad D_0=D,\\quad\n\\mathcal B_0=\\mathcal B,\\quad\n\\mathcal C=\\mathcal C_{\\mathrm{comp}},\\quad T_0=T',\\quad U_0=U.\n\\]\nProposition 18 verifies the ring assumptions. Proposition 19 gives exactness, the free generator, and nonzero torsion homology. Proposition 22 gives the homology-to-dual-homology implication for every global map into the thick closure, including tower tails.</p><p>The full thick category \\(\\mathcal C_{\\mathrm{comp}}\\) contains \\(T'\\), so its thick closure of \\(T'\\) is the same as in \\(\\mathcal D(\\mathcal N)\\). Proposition 17 excludes membership.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 24", "orig": "Theorem 7.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a homology-compatible triangulated equivalence \\( \\mathcal F:h\\mathrm{Sp}_E\\to\\mathcal D(\\mathcal M) \\) whose sheaf homology is naturally the descended, regraded \\(E_*(-)\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p&gt;8\\), \\(D=2(p-1)&gt;12\\), and \\(E=E(2)\\) in the Hazewinkel chart.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal D(E_*E)\\) be the stable localization of differential graded \\(E_*E\\)-comodules at homology isomorphisms.</p><p class=\"li\">Regrade a differential comodule \\(M\\) by\n\\[\n(C_j)_s=M_{j+sD}.\n\\]\nThis identifies its differential with \\(C_j\\to C_{j-1}\\) and its periodicity with \\(C_{j+D}=C_j(1)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a homology-compatible triangulated equivalence\n\\[\n\\mathcal F:h\\mathrm{Sp}_E\\to\\mathcal D(\\mathcal M)\n\\]\nwhose sheaf homology is naturally the descended, regraded \\(E_*(-)\\). Its suspension structure gives natural isomorphisms\n\\[\n\\mathcal F(\\Sigma^jZ)\\cong(\\mathcal FZ)[j]\n\\qquad(j\\in\\mathbb Z).\n\\]\nThe comparison is the Goerss–Hopkins construction of Pstrągowski [27, v2], with the inputs justified in Lemma 24.1.</p>", "proof": "<p>The regrading is invertible: write total degree \\(n=r+sD\\), \\(0\\leq r&lt;D\\), and recover \\(M_n=(C_r)_s\\). At the residue boundary, \\(C_{-1}=C_{D-1}(-1)\\) gives the correct degree-lowering differential. It preserves homology, quasi-isomorphisms, signed shifts, and cones.</p><p>Follow [27, v2]. Its topological and algebraic stage-zero reductions are respectively \\(E\\)-homology and differential-comodule homology. The comparison of hearts and the preferred unit/free-algebra map commute with stage-zero reduction and restrict to potential stages.</p><p>Lemma 24.1 provides dimension \\(d=6\\) and convergence. Stabilization at homotopy level two occurs at \\(\\ell=d+2-1=7\\). In the stated range \\(p&gt;8\\), the construction gives\n\\[\nh_2\\mathrm{Sp}_E\n\\simeq h_2\\mathcal M_7^{\\mathrm{top}}\n\\simeq h_2\\mathcal M_7^{\\mathrm{alg}}\n\\simeq h_2\\mathcal D(E_*E).\n\\]\nChoose an inverse to the final leg and regrade. Stage-zero compatibility gives the homology identification.</p><p>For the triangulated structure, use Appendix B of [27, v2] with the paper’s local correction. For a span \\(\\sigma=(B\\leftarrow A\\to C)\\) in \\(h_2\\mathcal T\\), the cone space at \\(W\\) is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Map}(B,W)\\times^h_{\\operatorname{Map}(A,W)}\n\\operatorname{Map}(C,W),\n\\]\nretaining its \\(2\\)-cell. The mapping space from a cone \\(q_c\\) with vertex \\(V\\) to any cone \\(q\\) is the homotopy fiber over \\(q\\) of\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Map}(V,W)\\to\\operatorname{Cone}_\\sigma(W).\n\\]\nFor a \\(1\\)-cocartesian cone this comparison is \\(1\\)-connected, so the fiber is nonempty and connected. Lifting the span to the stable infinity-category and forming a pushout gives existence. This supplies the needed existence and uniqueness of cone maps. The same arbitrary-target uniqueness constructs suspension maps naturally. The Appendix B recovery of triangles then makes the induced equivalence triangulated.</p><p>No monoidal or stable infinity-category equivalence is used. All limits and gluing were formed algebraically before realization.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 24.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(\\mathcal C_E=\\operatorname{Comod}_{E_*E}\\) be the ordinary abelian category of all graded comodules.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\mathcal C_E=\\operatorname{Comod}_{E_*E}\\) be the ordinary abelian category of all graded comodules. Then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ext}^s_{\\mathcal C_E}(M,N)=0\n\\qquad(s&gt;6)\n\\]\nfor arbitrary sources, targets, and internal shifts.</p><p>Let \\(\\mathrm{Syn}_E\\) be the Grothendieck prestable infinity-category of spherical hypercomplete sheaves on the site of finite spectra \\(P\\) with \\(E_*P\\) projective, with covers surjective on \\(E\\)-homology. Then\n\\[\nX\\simeq\\varprojlim_iX_{\\leq i}.\n\\]\nThe ordinary unbounded derived category \\(\\mathcal D_{\\mathrm{ord}}(\\mathcal C_E)\\) is left complete, and the topological and algebraic potential-stage categories satisfy\n\\[\n\\mathcal M_\\infty^{\\mathrm{top}}\n\\simeq\\varprojlim_\\ell\\mathcal M_\\ell^{\\mathrm{top}},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal M_\\infty^{\\mathrm{alg}}\n\\simeq\\varprojlim_\\ell\\mathcal M_\\ell^{\\mathrm{alg}}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\nB=E_*=\\mathbb Z_{(p)}[v_1^{\\mathrm{Haz}},(v_2^{\\mathrm{Haz}})^{\\pm1}],\n\\quad\nI_0=0,\\ I_1=(p),\\ I_2=(p,v_1^{\\mathrm{Haz}}),\n\\quad Q_h=B/I_h.\n\\]\nDegree phases split comodules into \\(D\\) copies of quasi-coherent sheaves on \\(\\mathcal M\\).</p><p>For targets annihilated by \\(I_h\\), the unit-source bounds are\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ext}^a_{\\mathcal C_E}(B,V)=0\n\\qquad(a&gt;6-h).\n\\]\nFor \\(h=0,1\\), these are Proposition 5 and closed-base compatibility. For \\(h=2\\), extend to \\(k\\); Lemma 1.1 identifies \\(v_1^{\\mathrm{Haz}}\\bmod p\\) with \\(t\\). Both vertical kernel and cokernel remain killed by \\(t\\). Proposition 5 and the sections bound three on \\(\\mathcal N/(t)\\) give total bound four through the two-row composite spectral sequence. Scalar descent detects vanishing.</p><p>The Hopf algebroid is flat Adams by Hovey’s topological-flatness and Landweber base-change results [16]. Dualizable comodules generate and are exactly those finite projective over \\(B\\). Resolve \\(Q_h\\) by dualizable epimorphisms for \\(h\\) steps. Its underlying \\(B\\)-projective dimension is \\(h\\), so dimension shifting makes the final kernel \\(B\\)-projective and dualizable.</p><p>For the resulting bounded dualizable resolution \\(P^\\bullet\\to Q_h\\), internal derived Hom into \\(V\\) is\n\\[\n(P^\\bullet)^\\vee\\otimes_BV.\n\\]\nIts cohomology is \\(\\operatorname{Ext}_B^b(Q_h,V)\\), lies in \\(0\\leq b\\leq h\\), and is killed by \\(I_h\\). The bounded hypercohomology spectral sequence, with the supported unit bounds, gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ext}^{&gt;6}_{\\mathcal C_E}(Q_h,V)=0.\n\\]\nLandweber filtration [18] gives this bound for every source finitely presented over \\(B\\).</p><p>These finite sources form a quotient-closed class detecting nonzero comodules, by the primitive theorem. Use the partial-section criterion: if \\(K\\) is injective and \\(K\\to L\\) is surjective on Hom from this class, it splits. A maximal partial section exists by exact filtered unions. If its domain is not all of \\(L\\), take a nonzero map from a finite source into the quotient; its image is still in the class. Extend the partial map by injectivity, then subtract a lift of its discrepancy to enlarge the section, a contradiction.</p><p>For an injective resolution of any target \\(N\\), the map \\(I^6\\to Z^7\\) is surjective on those Hom groups because \\(\\operatorname{Ext}^7(U,N)=0\\). It splits, so the kernel is injective and the resolution truncates at six. This proves the all-pairs bound.</p><p>For completeness, use the synthetic construction of [28] on the stated \\(E(2)\\)-site. Its colimit generators are \\(G_P=\\nu(L_EP)\\). For the Postnikov layer \\(X[i]\\),\n\\[\n\\pi_j\\operatorname{Map}(G_P,X[i])\n\\cong\n\\operatorname{Ext}^{i-j}_{\\mathcal C_E}(E_*P,\\pi_iX),\n\\]\nwhich vanishes for \\(i&gt;6+j\\). Mapping homotopy groups therefore stabilize uniformly. Representable mapping spaces preserve limits; the Milnor sequence gives the mapping-space equivalences required by the completeness argument of [28, Theorem 7.4]. Separatedness yields \\(X\\simeq\\varprojlim X_{\\leq i}\\). The same finite homological dimension gives left completeness of the ordinary derived category.</p><p>Finally the formal module-category limit statement of [28, Proposition 3.2], restricted to potential stages by its Definition 4.1 and Remark 4.4, supplies the two tower equivalences. Periodic unit modules identify with \\(\\mathrm{Sp}_E\\), and the algebraic side uses the periodicity algebra of [27, v2].</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 25", "orig": "Proposition 7.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( BP_*b\\cong\\Gamma_P\\otimes_{\\eta_R,P}B_2. \\) Landweber base change to \\(E(2)\\) and descent identify this comodule with \\(T_{\\mathbb Z}\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use an \\(\\mathbb E_4\\)-multiplication on \\(BP\\), so its module category is monoidal.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(b\\) be the successive regular scalar quotient by \\(v_i\\), \\(i&gt;2\\), followed by the sequential colimit. Then\n\\[\n\\pi_*b=B_2=\\mathbb Z_{(p)}[v_1,v_2].\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nBP_*b\\cong\\Gamma_P\\otimes_{\\eta_R,P}B_2.\n\\]\nLandweber base change to \\(E(2)\\) and descent identify this comodule with \\(T_{\\mathbb Z}\\). The corresponding comodule for \\(b_p^{\\wedge}\\) is \\(T_{\\mathbb Z_p}\\). These are \\(p\\)-monic and reduce modulo \\(p\\) to \\(T_0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The \\(\\mathbb E_4\\)-enhancement has even homotopy and a complex orientation. Thom classes give a multiplicative homotopy-category map from \\(MU_{(p)}\\); composing the Quillen summand gives a homotopy ring map from usual \\(BP\\). Finite even-cell skeleta of \\(MU_{(p)}\\) and its double smash have no suspended maps to the even target, so the Milnor \\(\\lim^1\\)-terms vanish and the maps and multiplicativity pass without phantom ambiguities.</p><p>This map is an equivalence: its mod-\\(p\\) cohomology map is a unit in degree zero; the Steenrod module is cyclic there with matching finite degreewise dimensions. The cofiber is connective finite type, and lowest-degree Hurewicz and Nakayama make it zero. Thus usual coefficient and cooperation formulas may be used for the enhanced module constructions.</p><p>There is a relative smash identification\n\\[\nBP\\wedge b\\simeq(BP\\wedge BP)\\otimes_{BP}b.\n\\]\nAs a right \\(BP\\)-module, \\(BP\\wedge BP\\) is a wedge of free shifts, obtained by lifting its homotopy basis. Flat Künneth gives the displayed comodule, and inserted units give its usual coproduct coaction.</p><p>Landweber base change gives\n\\[\nA_{\\mathrm{Haz}}\\otimes_{P,\\eta_L}\\Gamma_P\n\\otimes_{P,\\eta_R}B_2,\n\\]\nthe functions on strict arrows with a chosen left chart law and a right \\(\\mathcal X\\)-law. Descent is arrow composition, exactly the pullback of \\(f_*\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal X}\\).</p><p>The completion is the limit of \\(b/p^a\\), a homotopy-surjective tower, with degreewise coefficients \\(B_2\\otimes\\mathbb Z_p\\). It remains a \\(BP\\)-module and has the same mod-\\(p\\) cofiber. The same free-right-module calculation gives \\(T_{\\mathbb Z_p}\\). Monicity and reduction are checked on the affine chart.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 26", "orig": "Proposition 7.4 and the form identification in Remark 7.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For the quotient \\(b\\) of Proposition 25 at an odd prime, \\( H^*(b;\\mathbb F_p)\\cong \\mathcal A//E(Q_0,Q_1,Q_2), \\) where \\(Q_i\\) are Milnor primitives.", "statement": "<p>For the quotient \\(b\\) of Proposition 25 at an odd prime,\n\\[\nH^*(b;\\mathbb F_p)\\cong\n\\mathcal A//E(Q_0,Q_1,Q_2),\n\\]\nwhere \\(Q_i\\) are Milnor primitives.</p><p>Consequently \\(b_p^{\\wedge}\\) is a standard form of \\(BP\\langle2\\rangle_p^{\\wedge}\\). The odd-prime uniqueness results cited in the paper identify its underlying spectrum with the standard one; independence of standard quotient generators concerns underlying spectra, not necessarily their \\(BP\\)-module structures.</p>", "proof": "<p>Connective module truncation and reduction give \\(b\\to H\\mathbb F_p\\). For \\(N=b,H\\mathbb F_p\\), use the connective Künneth spectral sequence for\n\\[\nH\\mathbb F_p\\otimes_{BP}(BP\\wedge N).\n\\]\nThe map on Tor terms is the inclusion\n\\[\n\\mathbb F_p[t_1,t_2,\\ldots]\\otimes\\Lambda(\\epsilon_i:i&gt;2)\n\\longrightarrow\n\\mathbb F_p[t_1,t_2,\\ldots]\\otimes\\Lambda(\\epsilon_i:i\\geq0),\n\\qquad |\\epsilon_i|=2p^i-1.\n\\]\nUse Koszul resolutions on \\(\\eta_Rv_i\\), and on \\(p,\\eta_Rv_i\\), respectively. They are free over the left base, and tensoring with left \\(\\mathbb F_p\\) kills all differentials.</p><p>The target collapses by Milnor’s dual Steenrod algebra dimensions. Injectivity and naturality force source collapse page by page. Connectivity gives convergence and injection on mod-\\(p\\) homology. Dually \\(\\mathcal A\\) surjects onto \\(H^*(b;\\mathbb F_p)\\). There are no classes in the odd degrees of \\(Q_0,Q_1,Q_2\\), so they kill the degree-zero generator. The resulting quotient is an isomorphism by dimensions.</p><p>The quotient is connective and degreewise finite type over \\(\\mathbb Z_{(p)}\\). Apply Angeltveit–Lind uniqueness with Lee’s corrected proof and odd-prime result [20]. Lemma 1.1 records the coordinate congruence used for characteristic-\\(p\\) reduction.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 27", "orig": "Proposition 7.7", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(j\\in\\mathbb Z\\), \\( \\mathcal F(\\Sigma^jL_ES^0)\\cong\\mathcal O[j], \\qquad \\mathcal F(\\Sigma^jL_Eb)\\cong T_{\\mathbb Z}[j], \\qquad \\mathcal F(\\Sigma^jL_Eb_p^{\\wedge})\\cong T_{\\mathbb Z_p}[j]. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(p&gt;8\\) and the equivalence \\(\\mathcal F\\) of Proposition 24.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(b\\) be the quotient of Proposition 25.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(j\\in\\mathbb Z\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal F(\\Sigma^jL_ES^0)\\cong\\mathcal O[j],\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal F(\\Sigma^jL_Eb)\\cong T_{\\mathbb Z}[j],\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal F(\\Sigma^jL_Eb_p^{\\wedge})\\cong T_{\\mathbb Z_p}[j].\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The sphere’s homology is pure \\(\\mathcal O\\), of projective dimension at most six by Proposition 5. Since \\(D&gt;12\\), Lemma 27.1 applies.</p><p>The chart sheaves have injective dimension at most five. Resolve \\(\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal X}\\) by the length-two Čech resolution for its three principal opens. Each localized regular polynomial ring has global dimension at most three. The bounds apply to arbitrary graded modules: grading-preserving Hom is a summand of ordinary Hom by taking matching-degree components. Flat affine pushforward preserves injective-dimension bounds. Combining the length-two resolution with three gives five.</p><p>The same applies after tensoring the chart sheaf with \\(\\mathbb Z_p\\), since the bounds hold for arbitrary modules over these localized rings. Proposition 25 identifies the homology sheaves, and the injective-dimension case of Lemma 27.1 identifies the objects. Suspension compatibility supplies every shift.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 27.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In a twisted periodic derived category of period \\(D\\), suppose \\(C\\) has homology in only one residue modulo \\(D\\), with sheaf \\(W\\) in a chosen representative.", "statement": "<p>In a twisted periodic derived category of period \\(D\\), suppose \\(C\\) has homology in only one residue modulo \\(D\\), with sheaf \\(W\\) in a chosen representative. Then \\(C\\) is isomorphic to the corresponding pure object if either:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(\\operatorname{pd}(W)\\leq a\\) in the Ext sense and \\(D&gt;a+1\\); or</p><p class=\"li num\">2. \\(\\operatorname{id}(W)\\leq b\\) and \\(D&gt;b+1\\).</p><p>The assertion holds after every shift.</p>", "proof": "<p>Shift to residue zero. For the projective-dimension case, take a fibrant \\(C\\) with injective terms. Lifting \\(W\\to H_0C\\) to cycles is obstructed by \\(\\operatorname{Ext}^1(W,B_0C)\\). In positive intervening degrees, homology vanishes, and\n\\[\n0\\to B_jC\\to C_j\\to B_{j-1}C\\to0\n\\]\ndimension-shifts the obstruction past \\(a\\) before the next homology residue. The lift repeats periodically and is a quasi-isomorphism from pure \\(W\\).</p><p>For injective dimension, resolve \\(W\\) in degrees \\(0,-1,\\ldots,-b\\) and periodize. Map \\(C\\) into it: extend the homology isomorphism to \\(C_0/B_0C\\) by injectivity, then successively extend prescribed maps on boundaries in negative degrees. Vanishing intervening homology ensures the chain-map equations. The resolution ends before the next homology residue, so periodic copies do not interfere. The map is a quasi-isomorphism.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 28", "orig": "Lemma 8.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is an exact functor \\( Li^*:\\mathcal D(\\mathcal M)\\to\\mathcal D(\\mathcal M_0) \\) which is ordinary reduction on termwise \\(p\\)-monic complexes.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A sheaf or complex term is \\(p\\)-monic if multiplication by \\(p\\) is injective.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(i:\\mathcal M_0\\to\\mathcal M\\) be characteristic-\\(p\\) inclusion.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an exact functor\n\\[\nLi^*:\\mathcal D(\\mathcal M)\\to\\mathcal D(\\mathcal M_0)\n\\]\nwhich is ordinary reduction on termwise \\(p\\)-monic complexes. For \\(F\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathcal M_0)\\), there is a natural triangle\n\\[\nF[1]\\to Li^*i_*F\\to F\\xrightarrow{\\delta_F}F[2].\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>On the whole graded \\(BP\\)-stack, the cofree comodule \\(\\Gamma_P\\) is a union of finite-free dualizable subcomodules: take left spans of arrow monomials of bounded weight. Homogeneity and nonnegative weights make their coproducts stable.</p><p>A homogeneous element of a comodule \\(M\\) corresponds to a shifted-unit map into the diagonal cofree tensor \\(\\Gamma_P\\otimes_PM\\). Its finite coaction expression factors through \\(N\\otimes_PM\\) for such a finite-free subcomodule \\(N\\). The inclusions split on left modules, so duality gives a shifted \\(N^\\vee\\to M\\) whose image contains the original element. Summing gives an epimorphism from sums of dualizables.</p><p>For sheaves on the height-open \\(\\mathcal M\\), push forward to the whole stack, take such an epimorphism, and restrict. Quasi-compact open pushforward is quasi-coherent on flat charts. The restricted dualizables are \\(p\\)-monic.</p><p>Disk sums give a functorial degreewise epimorphism \\(P_C\\to C\\) with kernel \\(K_C\\), both termwise \\(p\\)-monic. The cone \\(K_C\\to P_C\\) is quasi-isomorphic to \\(C\\). Acyclic \\(p\\)-monic complexes remain acyclic after reduction by the short exact multiplication sequence. The \\(p\\)-monic subcategory has the same localization, so reduction defines the exact functor.</p><p>For \\(C=i_*F\\), the multiplication snake sequence is\n\\[\n0\\to F\\to K_C/p\\to P_C/p\\to F\\to0.\n\\]\nIts middle cone represents \\(Li^*i_*F\\), giving the triangle. The boundary is natural for chain maps and descends to localization.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 29", "orig": "Lemma 8.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Uniform shiftwise nilpotence is a thick property.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\delta:\\mathrm{id}\\to[2]\\) be natural on a triangulated category.</p><p class=\"li\">Say \\(A\\) has uniform shiftwise nilpotence if some \\(N\\geq1\\) makes\n\\[\n\\delta_{A[j]}^N:A[j]\\to A[j+2N]\n\\]\nzero for every \\(j\\). No compatibility of \\(\\delta_{A[j]}\\) with \\(\\delta_A[j]\\) is assumed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Uniform shiftwise nilpotence is a thick property.</p><p>If, additionally, the cones of \\(\\delta_{A[j]}\\) are shifts of \\(B(A[j])\\) for an exact functor \\(B\\), then an object with this property belongs to \\(\\operatorname{Thick}(B(A))\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Shifts and retracts preserve the property. For a triangle \\(A\\to B_1\\to C\\), let \\(a,c\\) work uniformly for \\(A,C\\). Naturality makes the \\(c\\)-fold power on \\(B_1[j]\\), followed by the map to \\(C[j+2c]\\), zero. Exactness factors it through \\(A[j+2c]\\). Another \\(a\\) applications kill that factorization by naturality. Thus \\(a+c\\) works.</p><p>Each factor in \\(\\delta_A^N\\) has cone in \\(\\operatorname{Thick}(B(A))\\). Repeated octahedra put the composite cone there. If the composite is zero, its cone is \\(A[2N]\\oplus A[1]\\). Retract and shift give membership.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 30", "orig": "Lemma 8.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\widehat F\\in\\operatorname{Thick}(\\widehat T_0). \\) The transformations \\(\\epsilon\\) on \\(U\\) and \\(\\delta\\) on \\(F\\) are uniformly nilpotent over all shifts.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(U\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathcal N)\\) and\n\\[\n\\widehat U\\in\\operatorname{Thick}(\\widehat T').\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nF=e_*r^*U\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathcal M_0).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\epsilon_U:U\\to U[2]\\) be the shifted boundary of Proposition 6, and let \\(\\delta_F\\) be the reduction boundary of Proposition 28.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\widehat F\\in\\operatorname{Thick}(\\widehat T_0).\n\\]\nThe transformations \\(\\epsilon\\) on \\(U\\) and \\(\\delta\\) on \\(F\\) are uniformly nilpotent over all shifts.</p>", "proof": "<p>The fiber of \\(U\\to\\widehat U\\) is \\(t\\)-invertible; pullback preserves this and completion kills it. Therefore\n\\[\n\\widehat{r^*U}\\simeq\\widehat{r^*(\\widehat U)}.\n\\]\nThe exact completed-pullback functor reduces the assertion to \\(T'\\). At each finite level coinduction gives\n\\[\n0\\to r^*T'_d\\to T_{k,d}\\xrightarrow{c-1}T_{k,d}\\to0.\n\\]\nFor surjectivity, refine a finite quotient until every central period increases by a multiple of \\(p\\). This is possible because the center embeds profinitely and its \\(p\\)-power images meet tame images trivially. Repeated function values sum to zero in characteristic \\(p\\), so integrate as a difference. The kernel is the rigidified coinduction.</p><p>Taking homotopy limits expresses \\(\\widehat{r^*T'}\\) by two copies of \\(\\widehat T_k\\). Thus \\(\\widehat{r^*U}\\in\\operatorname{Thick}(\\widehat T_k)\\), without assuming pullback commutes with arbitrary limits. Finite-field restriction commutes with limits and sends \\(\\widehat T_k\\) to finitely many copies of \\(\\widehat T_0\\).</p><p>On the open, Proposition 7 splits every object into finitely many pure period residues, each having no self-map to its \\(2\\)-shift. Naturality against inclusions makes both boundaries zero on all open objects and shifts. Proposition 8 gives the same self-map vanishing on complete generators. Proposition 29 makes nilpotence hold on their thick closures.</p><p>Apply this to the fracture triangles\n\\[\nU\\to\\widehat U\\oplus LU\\to L\\widehat U,\n\\qquad\nF\\to\\widehat F\\oplus LF\\to L\\widehat F.\n\\]\nThe complete terms are nilpotent and the open terms have zero boundaries, giving uniform nilpotence on \\(U,F\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 31", "orig": "Lemma 8.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assume \\(p>8\\) and \\(U\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathcal N)\\) satisfies \\( \\pi_iU\\text{ finite for all }i,\\qquad \\widehat U\\in\\operatorname{Thick}(\\widehat T'),\\qquad U\\notin\\operatorname{Thick}(T'). \\) Then …", "statement": "<p>Assume \\(p&gt;8\\) and \\(U\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathcal N)\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\pi_iU\\text{ finite for all }i,\\qquad\n\\widehat U\\in\\operatorname{Thick}(\\widehat T'),\\qquad\nU\\notin\\operatorname{Thick}(T').\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nLi^*(i_*e_*r^*U)\\notin\\operatorname{Thick}(T_0).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(F=e_*r^*U\\). If the displayed object belonged to \\(\\operatorname{Thick}(T_0)\\), the reduction triangle, Proposition 30, and Proposition 29 would give \\(F\\in\\operatorname{Thick}(T_0)\\).</p><p>Exact scalar extension then gives\n\\[\ne^*F\\in\\operatorname{Thick}(T_k).\n\\]\nSeparability makes \\(r^*U\\) a retract of \\(e^*e_*r^*U\\), so it belongs to the same thick closure. Apply the exact \\(Q\\) of Proposition 6, with \\(QT_k=T'\\). Then \\(Qr^*U\\in\\operatorname{Thick}(T')\\). Central-boundary nilpotence and Proposition 29 imply \\(U\\in\\operatorname{Thick}(T')\\), a contradiction.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 32", "orig": "Proposition 8.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The spectrum \\(X_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\) is connective, \\(p\\)-complete, fp, and of fp-type at most two, with …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p&gt;8\\), \\(D&gt;12\\), and choose Proposition 24’s comparison.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(U\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathcal N)\\) satisfies the three hypotheses of Proposition 31.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(Z_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\in\\mathrm{Sp}_{E(2)}\\) with\n\\[\n\\mathcal FZ_{\\mathrm{sp}}\n=\n\\widetilde U=i_*e_*r^*U.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(X_{\\mathrm{sp}}=\\tau_{\\geq0}Z_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The spectrum \\(X_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\) is connective, \\(p\\)-complete, fp, and of fp-type at most two, with\n\\[\nL_{E(2)}X_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\simeq Z_{\\mathrm{sp}},\n\\qquad\nX_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\notin\n\\operatorname{Thick}_{\\mathrm{Sp}_p^{\\wedge}}(b_p^{\\wedge}).\n\\]\nEvery \\(\\pi_iZ_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\) is a finite \\(p\\)-group.</p>", "proof": "<p>Essential surjectivity chooses \\(Z_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\). Proposition 27 identifies\n\\[\n\\pi_iZ_{\\mathrm{sp}}\n\\cong[\\mathcal O[i],i_*e_*r^*U]\n\\cong\\pi_iQr^*U.\n\\]\nThe last computation uses the coinduced acyclic resolution, closed-base compatibility, and finite sections bounds. Proposition 6 and degreewise finite \\(\\pi_iU\\) make these finite \\(p\\)-groups. The identifications are additive.</p><p>The connective cover has finite \\(p\\)-group homotopy. Its finite Postnikov stages are \\(p\\)-complete, and \\(p\\)-complete spectra are closed under limits, so \\(X_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\) is \\(p\\)-complete.</p><p>The spectrum \\(H\\mathbb F_p\\) is \\(E(2)\\)-acyclic. Its \\(BP\\)-module calculation is\n\\[\nBP_*H\\mathbb F_p=\\Gamma_P\\otimes_{\\eta_R,P}\\mathbb F_p.\n\\]\nThe right-unit congruences make left positive coefficient actions vanish successively; inverting \\(v_2\\) kills the comodule. The tail \\(\\tau_{&lt;0}Z_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\) is the filtered colimit of finite Postnikov ranges. Composition series of their finite \\(p\\)-groups express each range as a finite construction from shifts of \\(H\\mathbb F_p\\). Thus the tail is \\(E(2)\\)-acyclic, proving the localization assertion.</p><p>Let \\(V\\) be finite type three, whose existence follows from periodicity. Both \\(V\\) and its finite dual are \\(E(2)\\)-acyclic, using\n\\[\n\\langle E(2)\\rangle=\n\\langle K(0)\\vee K(1)\\vee K(2)\\rangle.\n\\]\nFor every \\(W\\),\n\\[\n[W,V\\wedge Z_{\\mathrm{sp}}]\n\\cong[DV\\wedge W,Z_{\\mathrm{sp}}]=0,\n\\]\nso \\(V\\wedge Z_{\\mathrm{sp}}=0\\). Hence\n\\[\nV\\wedge X_{\\mathrm{sp}}\n\\simeq\n\\Sigma^{-1}(V\\wedge\\tau_{&lt;0}Z_{\\mathrm{sp}}).\n\\]\nThe left side is bounded below and degreewise finite; the right side is bounded above because \\(V\\) has finitely many cells. Its homotopy is therefore finite in total. Mahowald–Rezk’s criterion proves fp-ness and fp-type at most two.</p><p>If \\(X_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\in\\operatorname{Thick}(b_p^{\\wedge})\\), exact localization and \\(\\mathcal F\\) give\n\\[\n\\widetilde U\\in\\operatorname{Thick}(T_{\\mathbb Z_p}).\n\\]\nThe generator is \\(p\\)-monic with reduction \\(T_0\\), so exact \\(Li^*\\) gives\n\\[\nLi^*\\widetilde U\\in\\operatorname{Thick}(T_0),\n\\]\ncontradicting Proposition 31.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 33", "orig": "Proposition 9.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(U\\) be the glued object of Proposition 16, and let \\(X_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\) be its connective spectrum from Proposition 32.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(U\\) be the glued object of Proposition 16, and let \\(X_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\) be its connective spectrum from Proposition 32. Then \\(X_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\) has exact fp-type two.</p>", "proof": "<p>If its fp-type were at most one, the height-one theorem of Lee–Pstrągowski would give\n\\[\nX_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\in\\operatorname{Thick}(BP\\langle1\\rangle_p^{\\wedge}).\n\\]</p><p>In the same monoidal \\(BP\\)-module category form\n\\[\nb_1=\\operatorname{cofib}\n(\\Sigma^{2(p^2-1)}b\\xrightarrow{v_2}b).\n\\]\nRegularity gives \\(\\pi_*b_1=\\mathbb Z_{(p)}[v_1]\\). The proof of Proposition 26 with cutoff one gives\n\\[\nH^*(b_1;\\mathbb F_p)\n=\\mathcal A//E(Q_0,Q_1).\n\\]\nIt uses the same injective Koszul comparison and Milnor dimensions. The connective finite-type uniqueness theorem identifies \\((b_1)_p^{\\wedge}\\) with \\(BP\\langle1\\rangle_p^{\\wedge}\\).</p><p>Exact completion of its cofiber sequence puts this generator in \\(\\operatorname{Thick}(b_p^{\\wedge})\\). The assumed height-one membership would contradict Proposition 32. The upper bound two is already known.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 34", "orig": "Proposition 9.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( L_2^fX_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\simeq L_2X_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\simeq Z_{\\mathrm{sp}}, \\qquad L_{T(2)}X_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\simeq L_{K(2)}X_{\\mathrm{sp}}. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(X_{\\mathrm{sp}}=\\tau_{\\geq0}Z_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\) from Proposition 32.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(T(j)\\), \\(j&gt;0\\), be telescopes of positive-degree \\(v_j\\)-self-maps of finite type-\\(j\\) spectra, and put \\(T(0)=H\\mathbb Q\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nL_2^fX_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\simeq L_2X_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\simeq Z_{\\mathrm{sp}},\n\\qquad\nL_{T(2)}X_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\simeq L_{K(2)}X_{\\mathrm{sp}}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(N=\\tau_{&lt;0}Z_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\). Each finite Postnikov range is a finite extension of shifts of \\(H\\mathbb F_p\\). For \\(j&gt;0\\), finite mod-\\(p\\) homology is bounded, so iterating a positive-degree self-map gives zero telescope on that homology. Thus\n\\[\nH\\mathbb F_p\\wedge T(j)=0.\n\\]\nRational acyclicity gives the same for \\(j=0\\). Hence \\(N\\) is \\(T(j)\\)-acyclic for \\(0\\leq j\\leq2\\).</p><p>Finite localization is smashing, with acyclics generated by a finite type-three spectrum. The Boolean algebra theorem identifies\n\\[\n\\langle L_2^fS^0\\rangle\n=\n\\langle T(0)\\vee T(1)\\vee T(2)\\rangle.\n\\]\nThus \\(N\\) is \\(L_2^f\\)-acyclic. Every \\(E(2)\\)-local object is \\(L_2^f\\)-local because the finite generators of the latter acyclics are \\(E(2)\\)-acyclic. The cofiber sequence \\(X_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\to Z_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\to N\\) proves the first equivalence; Proposition 32 gives the \\(L_2\\)-identification.</p><p>For the second, \\(T(2)\\)-acyclicity implies \\(K(2)\\)-acyclicity by the field Künneth theorem and nonzero \\(K(2)_*T(2)\\). Thus \\(K(2)\\)-local objects are \\(T(2)\\)-local, and it suffices to work with \\(Z_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\).</p><p>Let\n\\[\nG=\\operatorname{fib}(Z_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\to L_{K(2)}Z_{\\mathrm{sp}}).\n\\]\nIt is \\(E(2)\\)-local and \\(K(2)\\)-acyclic. Smashing \\(E(2)\\)-localization makes \\(T(2)\\wedge G\\) remain local. Its lower \\(K\\)-theories vanish because they vanish on \\(T(2)\\), and its \\(K(2)\\)-homology vanishes because it vanishes on \\(G\\). The Bousfield-class identity makes it also \\(E(2)\\)-acyclic, hence zero. The map is therefore a \\(T(2)\\)-equivalence to a \\(T(2)\\)-local target, proving the comparison.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 2, "cite"], [4, 6, "cite"], [5, 4, "cite"], [5, 50, "cite"], [5, 52, "cite"], [6, 8, "cite"], [7, 6, "cite"], [8, 16, "cite"], [8, 19, "cite"], [8, 28, "cite"], [9, 8, "cite"], [10, 8, "cite"], [12, 11, "cite"], [13, 17, "cite"], [13, 18, "cite"], [13, 24, "cite"], [13, 50, "cite"], [13, 53, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"], [14, 15, "cite"], [15, 13, "cite"], [16, 13, "cite"], [17, 57, "cite"], [17, 58, "cite"], [17, 59, "cite"], [18, 23, "cite"], [18, 25, "cite"], [18, 26, "cite"], [18, 35, "cite"], [18, 57, "cite"], [19, 18, "cite"], [20, 22, "cite"], [20, 23, "cite"], [20, 57, "cite"], [21, 20, "cite"], [22, 43, "cite"], [23, 26, "cite"], [23, 34, "cite"], [24, 23, "cite"], [24, 25, "cite"], [25, 23, "cite"], [26, 35, "cite"], [26, 46, "cite"], [27, 29, "cite"], [27, 30, "cite"], [27, 31, "cite"], [27, 44, "cite"], [28, 27, "cite"], [29, 0, "cite"], [29, 33, "cite"], [29, 36, "cite"], [30, 29, "cite"], [30, 31, "cite"], [31, 29, "cite"], [32, 29, "cite"], [33, 34, "cite"], [33, 36, "cite"], [35, 0, "cite"], [35, 43, "cite"], [35, 46, "cite"], [35, 48, "cite"], [35, 60, "cite"], [36, 46, "cite"], [37, 48, "cite"], [38, 37, "cite"], [38, 42, "cite"], [39, 37, "cite"], [40, 43, "cite"], [40, 45, "cite"], [40, 48, "cite"], [41, 40, "cite"], [42, 40, "cite"], [43, 46, "cite"], [43, 48, "cite"], [44, 46, "cite"], [45, 46, "cite"], [46, 48, "cite"], [47, 46, "cite"], [48, 0, "cite"], [48, 2, "cite"], [49, 53, "cite"], [49, 59, "cite"], [50, 49, "cite"], [51, 52, "cite"], [51, 53, "cite"], [52, 0, "cite"], [52, 60, "cite"], [53, 59, "cite"], [54, 53, "cite"], [55, 57, "cite"], [56, 57, "cite"], [56, 58, "cite"], [57, 58, "cite"], [58, 59, "cite"], [59, 0, "cite"], [59, 2, "cite"], [59, 60, "cite"], [59, 61, "cite"], [60, 0, "cite"], [60, 2, "cite"], [61, 0, "cite"], [61, 2, "cite"]]});
